window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"IN-1":{"unit":"IN-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01","title":"Skin Layers, Junctions, Dermis, and Adnexa","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter builds the skin from the surface down: the epidermal layers and the keratinocytes that mature through them, the resident melanocytes, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells, the junctions that hold keratinocytes to each other and to the basement membrane, the dermis and hypodermis, and the glands, hair, and nails. Board questions on skin usually describe a blister, a biopsy report, or a pattern of sweating or hair loss and ask which structure or protein has failed. If you know which protein holds each level together and what happens when it fails, you can predict the split level, the type of blister, how it heals, and the histologic term a pathologist would use.","prerequisites":["Epithelial tissue classification and the general structure of cell junctions","Intermediate filaments, actin, and the cadherin and integrin adhesion families","Collagen types I, III, IV, and VII and where each is found","Sympathetic transmitters and receptors, including the cholinergic sympathetic exception","Type II and type IV hypersensitivity and complement activation"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology","how":"Langerhans cells start the sensitization phase of allergic contact dermatitis, and pemphigus and pemphigoid are antibody-mediated (type II) injuries to adhesion proteins."},{"to":"Neuroscience","how":"Meissner, Pacinian, and Merkel endings feed large myelinated afferents into the dorsal column pathway, while free nerve endings feed the spinothalamic pathway."},{"to":"Biochemistry and genetics","how":"Melanin is made from tyrosine by tyrosinase, and mutations in keratins, laminin 332, and collagens XVII and VII cause the inherited epidermolysis bullosa groups."},{"to":"Endocrinology","how":"Androgens drive sebaceous gland growth and the miniaturization of scalp follicles, and puberty activates apocrine glands."},{"to":"Pulmonary and gastrointestinal pathophysiology","how":"Defective CFTR in the eccrine sweat duct produces the high sweat chloride used to diagnose cystic fibrosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Keratinocyte","def":"The main cell of the epidermis, which divides in the basal layer and matures into a dead, keratin-filled corneocyte as it moves to the surface."},{"term":"Corneocyte","def":"A flat, anucleate cell of the stratum corneum made of keratin filaments inside a cross-linked cornified envelope."},{"term":"Desmosome","def":"A spot junction that joins two cells through desmoglein and desmocollin cadherins and anchors keratin filaments on each side."},{"term":"Hemidesmosome","def":"A junction on the basal surface of basal keratinocytes that attaches their keratin filaments to the basement membrane."},{"term":"Basement membrane zone","def":"The layered junction between epidermis and dermis: lamina lucida, lamina densa, and sublamina densa anchoring fibrils."},{"term":"Epidermal melanin unit","def":"One melanocyte together with the group of keratinocytes, about 30 to 40, to which it supplies melanosomes."},{"term":"Acantholysis","def":"Loss of cohesion between keratinocytes, so that they separate and round up within the epidermis."},{"term":"Nikolsky sign","def":"Separation of the epidermis when apparently normal skin is rubbed sideways, seen when the split is within the epidermis."},{"term":"Holocrine secretion","def":"Secretion in which the whole cell fills with product and breaks down to release it, as in sebaceous glands."},{"term":"Pilosebaceous unit","def":"A hair follicle with its sebaceous gland and arrector pili muscle, the unit involved in acne."},{"term":"Bulge","def":"The region of the outer root sheath at the arrector pili insertion that holds the follicle's stem cells."},{"term":"Telogen effluvium","def":"Diffuse, nonscarring hair shedding that begins about 2 to 3 months after a stressor pushes many follicles into telogen at once."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C01","obj":"IN-1.1","title":"Epidermal layers and keratinocyte maturation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The epidermis is a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with no blood vessels; it is fed by diffusion from capillary loops in the dermal papillae. Most of its cells are keratinocytes, which are born in the deepest layer and pushed toward the surface. Each layer is therefore one stage in the life of the same cell, and its name describes how that stage looks under the microscope."},{"type":"p","text":"The stratum basale is a single row of dividing stem and transit-amplifying cells on the basement membrane. They make keratins 5 and 14, a type II and type I pair that forms a flexible filament network anchored to hemidesmosomes below and desmosomes at the sides. As daughter cells enter the stratum spinosum they switch to keratins 1 and 10 and build thicker filament bundles that insert into many desmosomes. When cells shrink during tissue processing these junctions stretch into the short projections that give the layer its name."},{"type":"p","text":"In the stratum granulosum the cells assemble the barrier. Basophilic keratohyalin granules hold profilaggrin and loricrin; profilaggrin is cleaved to filaggrin, which packs keratin filaments into dense bundles, and its breakdown products help the corneum hold water. Lamellar bodies discharge lipid between the cells, where enzymes convert it to ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids that form sheets blocking water loss. Transglutaminase cross-links involucrin, loricrin, and other proteins beneath the plasma membrane into the cornified envelope, and the cell then destroys its nucleus and organelles in a specialized form of programmed death."},{"type":"p","text":"The stratum corneum is made of flat, anucleate corneocytes set in lipid, often described as bricks and mortar. Proteases slowly digest the remaining desmosomes so single cells shed from the surface, and the trip from basal layer to shedding takes about 4 weeks. Thick skin of the palms and soles adds a thin, pale-staining stratum lucidum between the granulosum and corneum, has a far thicker corneum, and lacks hair follicles."},{"type":"table","title":"Epidermal layers, deep to surface","cols":["Layer","Keratinocyte state","Key molecules and structures"],"rows":[["Stratum basale","Single row of dividing cells on the basement membrane","Keratins 5 and 14; hemidesmosomes"],["Stratum spinosum","Several rows of polygonal cells joined by many desmosomes","Keratins 1 and 10; desmosomes"],["Stratum granulosum","Flattening cells assembling the barrier","Keratohyalin granules (profilaggrin, loricrin); lamellar bodies"],["Stratum lucidum","Thin, clear band; thick skin only","Densely packed keratin"],["Stratum corneum","Flat dead corneocytes, no nuclei","Cornified envelope; intercellular lipid sheets"]],"caption":"Surface to deep, the order is corneum, lucidum (thick skin only), granulosum, spinosum, basale."},{"type":"flow","title":"Keratinocyte maturation, basale to corneum","steps":["Basal stem cell divides, expressing keratins 5 and 14","Daughter cell enters spinosum and switches to keratins 1 and 10","Granulosum: keratohyalin granules form, lamellar bodies release lipid","Cornified envelope cross-linked; nucleus and organelles degraded","Anucleate corneocyte reaches the surface and is shed"],"caption":"The whole journey takes about 4 weeks in normal skin and only a few days in psoriasis."},{"type":"p","text":"A defect at each stage produces a distinct disease. Keratin 5 or 14 mutations make basal cells fragile (epidermolysis bullosa simplex, C04), and keratin 1 or 10 mutations make suprabasal cells fragile (epidermolytic ichthyosis). Loss-of-function filaggrin mutations weaken the barrier, causing ichthyosis vulgaris and raising the risk of atopic dermatitis. In psoriasis, transit shortens so much that cells reach the surface with nuclei still present (C08)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If lamellar bodies could not deliver their lipids but keratin and the cornified envelope formed normally, how would the skin behave?","explain":"Corneocytes would form, but without lipid sheets between them water would leak out freely. Transepidermal water loss rises and the skin becomes dry, scaly, and easily penetrated. Harlequin ichthyosis, caused by ABCA12 mutations that block lipid loading of lamellar bodies, is the severe inherited form.","check":"The stratum granulosum paragraph and the bricks and mortar description of the corneum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C02","obj":"IN-1.2","title":"Melanocytes, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three non-keratinocyte cells live in the epidermis, and each sits where its job requires. They are hard to tell apart on routine stains, so boards identify them by layer, origin, marker, and function."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanocytes arise from neural crest cells that migrate to the skin during embryonic life and settle in the stratum basale, roughly 1 for every 4 to 10 basal keratinocytes depending on region. They attach to the basement membrane but not to keratinocytes by desmosomes, so they look like pale clear cells on routine stains. Inside membrane-bound melanosomes, tyrosinase converts tyrosine to melanin. Melanocytes then extend dendrites between keratinocytes, and the keratinocytes take up the melanosome-filled tips. In each keratinocyte the melanin gathers over the sun-facing side of the nucleus as a cap that absorbs ultraviolet light before it reaches DNA."},{"type":"flow","title":"Melanin synthesis and transfer","steps":["Tyrosinase oxidizes tyrosine to DOPA, then DOPA to dopaquinone","Dopaquinone forms eumelanin, or pheomelanin when cysteine is added","Melanosomes mature and move out along melanocyte dendrites","Keratinocytes engulf dendrite tips containing melanosomes","Melanin forms a cap over each keratinocyte nucleus"],"caption":"Alpha-MSH acting on the melanocortin 1 receptor favors brown-black eumelanin; ultraviolet light increases tyrosinase activity and transfer, producing a tan."},{"type":"p","text":"Skin color differs between people because of the number, size, melanin type, and breakdown rate of melanosomes, not because of melanocyte number, which is about the same in all skin colors. This explains two contrasting white-skin disorders. In oculocutaneous albinism type 1, melanocytes are present in normal numbers but tyrosinase is defective, so they cannot make pigment. In vitiligo, autoimmune destruction removes the melanocytes themselves."},{"type":"p","text":"Langerhans cells are dendritic antigen-presenting cells, mainly in the stratum spinosum, that are classically described as bone marrow derived. They express CD1a, langerin (CD207), and S-100, and on electron microscopy they contain rod-shaped Birbeck granules with a terminal bulb, the tennis-racket shape. They capture antigens and haptens, travel through dermal lymphatics to regional lymph nodes, and present them to naive T cells. This is the sensitization step of allergic contact dermatitis, a type IV reaction. Langerhans cell histiocytosis is a clonal proliferation of cells with the same markers."},{"type":"p","text":"Merkel cells sit in the stratum basale, joined to keratinocytes by desmosomes, and contain dense-core neurosecretory granules. Each contacts the expanded disc ending of a large myelinated sensory axon, forming a slowly adapting mechanoreceptor that signals sustained pressure, edges, and texture, so they are most numerous in fingertips and lips. Merkel cell carcinoma is an aggressive neuroendocrine skin cancer, often linked to Merkel cell polyomavirus."},{"type":"table","title":"Resident non-keratinocyte cells","cols":["Cell","Layer","Origin","Identifying features","Function"],"rows":[["Melanocyte","Stratum basale","Neural crest","Pale cell with dendrites; melanosomes; tyrosinase","Makes melanin and transfers it to keratinocytes"],["Langerhans cell","Mainly stratum spinosum","Bone marrow (classic teaching)","Birbeck granules; CD1a, langerin (CD207), S-100","Captures antigen and presents it in lymph nodes"],["Merkel cell","Stratum basale","Debated: epidermal or neural crest","Neurosecretory granules; contact with sensory disc endings","Slowly adapting touch receptor"]],"caption":"Two cells sit in the basal layer (melanocytes and Merkel cells); the antigen-presenting cell sits higher, in the spinosum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C03","obj":"IN-1.3","title":"Desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, adherens junctions, and autoimmune blistering","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The epidermis resists shearing because junctions tie the keratin network of every keratinocyte to its neighbors and to the basement membrane, turning the sheet into one mechanical unit. Remove any link and the tissue tears at that point, filling with fluid as a blister. The level of the tear identifies the protein that failed."},{"type":"p","text":"Desmosomes join keratinocyte to keratinocyte. Their calcium-dependent cadherins, desmogleins and desmocollins, bridge the space between cells, and a cytoplasmic plaque of plakoglobin, plakophilin, and desmoplakin anchors them to keratin. Desmoglein 3 is concentrated in the lower epidermis and in oral mucosa, while desmoglein 1 is concentrated in the upper epidermis and is scarce in mucosa. Adherens junctions also join neighboring cells, but through E-cadherin linked by catenins to actin; they organize the sheet and its signaling, and loss of E-cadherin lets carcinoma cells detach and invade. Hemidesmosomes join the basal cell to the basement membrane: integrin alpha6 beta4 binds laminin 332, the transmembrane protein BP180 (collagen XVII) reaches into the lamina lucida, and BP230 and plectin link these to keratins 5 and 14 inside the cell."},{"type":"table","title":"Keratinocyte junctions","cols":["Junction","Connects","Adhesion proteins","Cytoskeleton","Disease when targeted"],"rows":[["Desmosome","Cell to cell","Desmogleins 1 and 3, desmocollins","Keratin, through desmoplakin","Pemphigus vulgaris and foliaceus; staphylococcal scalded skin"],["Adherens junction","Cell to cell","E-cadherin","Actin, through catenins","Loss of E-cadherin aids carcinoma invasion"],["Hemidesmosome","Basal cell to basement membrane","BP180 (collagen XVII), BP230, integrin alpha6 beta4","Keratins 5 and 14, through plectin and BP230","Bullous pemphigoid; junctional epidermolysis bullosa"]],"caption":"Desmosome problems split the epidermis from within; hemidesmosome problems lift the whole epidermis off the basement membrane."},{"type":"p","text":"In pemphigus vulgaris, IgG autoantibodies against desmoglein 3, often with desmoglein 1, cause acantholysis just above the basal layer. The basal cells stay attached by their hemidesmosomes and line the floor in a tombstone row. The roof is thin, so blisters are flaccid and rupture into erosions, oral lesions are nearly universal, the Nikolsky sign is positive, and direct immunofluorescence shows IgG between keratinocytes in a net-like pattern. In pemphigus foliaceus, antibodies only against desmoglein 1 split the epidermis just under the stratum corneum, producing crusted superficial erosions without mouth involvement. Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1 at the same level, causing bullous impetigo and scalded skin syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"In bullous pemphigoid, IgG against BP180 and BP230 separates the entire epidermis from the basement membrane. The roof is full thickness, so blisters are tense, the Nikolsky sign is negative, mucosa is less often involved, and patients are usually older adults. Immunofluorescence shows linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane, and biopsies contain many eosinophils."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bullous pemphigoid pathogenesis","steps":["IgG autoantibodies bind BP180 and BP230 at hemidesmosomes","Bound antibody activates complement along the basement membrane","Mast cells degranulate; eosinophils and neutrophils are recruited","Released proteases cleave the hemidesmosome attachments","Subepidermal split forms a tense, fluid-filled blister"],"caption":"Complement and inflammatory cells, not antibody binding alone, cause most of the damage, which is why linear C3 accompanies IgG."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient whose antibodies target only desmoglein 1 have skin blisters but a normal mouth?","explain":"Oral mucosa contains abundant desmoglein 3 and little desmoglein 1, so intact desmoglein 3 keeps mucosal cells attached. In skin, the upper epidermis relies mainly on desmoglein 1, so it splits there. When antibodies to desmoglein 3 are added, the mucosa fails as well, as in pemphigus vulgaris.","check":"The distribution of desmogleins 1 and 3 and the pemphigus paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C04","obj":"IN-1.4","title":"The dermal-epidermal junction and the level of the split","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Below the basal keratinocyte lies the basement membrane zone, which electron microscopy resolves into three layers. The lamina lucida is a pale band crossed by anchoring filaments of laminin 332 and the extracellular part of collagen XVII, which tie hemidesmosomes downward. The lamina densa is a sheet of type IV collagen with nidogen and perlecan, the same network found in all basement membranes. Below it, anchoring fibrils of type VII collagen loop from the lamina densa into the papillary dermis and trap its type I and III collagen fibers."},{"type":"p","text":"Each component is a link in a chain, and the chain breaks at the weakest link. A blister therefore forms just above the structure that still holds, and its floor and roof tell you the level. Inherited defects (epidermolysis bullosa) and autoantibodies follow the same map."},{"type":"table","title":"Split level by failed component","cols":["Level of split","Failed protein","Inherited disease","Acquired disease","Healing"],"rows":[["Within basal keratinocytes","Keratin 5 or 14; plectin","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","None classic","Usually without scarring"],["Lamina lucida","Laminin 332, collagen XVII, integrin alpha6 beta4","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa","Bullous pemphigoid (BP180, BP230)","Usually without scarring; atrophy possible in junctional forms"],["Below the lamina densa","Type VII collagen","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Scarring and milia"]],"caption":"Deeper splits tear the papillary dermis, so they heal with scarring; splits above the lamina densa leave the basement membrane on the floor."},{"type":"p","text":"Depth explains the clinical picture. Splits at or above the lamina densa leave an intact basement membrane on which keratinocytes can migrate, so they usually heal without scars. Splits below it disrupt the papillary dermis, so they heal with scarring and milia (small keratin cysts), and the recessive form of dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa produces fused fingers and a high risk of squamous cell carcinoma in chronic wounds. Integrin alpha6 beta4 defects cause junctional disease with pyloric atresia because the same integrin anchors gut epithelium. Antibodies to type IV collagen do not blister the skin: anti-glomerular basement membrane disease targets the alpha3 chain, which is present in kidney and lung basement membranes but not in the epidermal lamina densa."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the split level to separate two lookalike diseases","text":"Two adults have tense subepidermal blisters with linear IgG along the basement membrane. One heals cleanly; the other heals with scars and milia on the hands and elbows. Scarring means a split below the lamina densa, so the second patient's antibodies target type VII collagen (epidermolysis bullosa acquisita), not BP180 or BP230. The lab can confirm this with salt-split skin: incubating normal skin in 1 M sodium chloride separates it through the lamina lucida. Bullous pemphigoid antibodies bind the roof (the hemidesmosome side), while antibodies to type VII collagen bind the floor (the dermal side)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C05","obj":"IN-1.5","title":"Papillary dermis, reticular dermis, and hypodermis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The dermis is the mesoderm-derived connective tissue that gives skin its strength, recoil, blood supply, and most of its sensation. It has two layers built for different loads, and beneath it the hypodermis (subcutis) joins skin to deeper fascia."},{"type":"p","text":"The papillary dermis is a thin layer of loose connective tissue with fine type III and type I collagen, thin elastic fibers, and many fibroblasts, mast cells, and macrophages. It rises into dermal papillae that interlock with the downward epidermal ridges (rete ridges), which enlarges the contact surface and resists shear. Capillary loops in each papilla nourish the avascular epidermis by diffusion, and in hairless (glabrous) skin many papillae hold a Meissner corpuscle."},{"type":"p","text":"The reticular dermis is much thicker dense irregular connective tissue. Coarse bundles of type I collagen give tensile strength, coarse elastic fibers give recoil, and the bases of hair follicles and glands sit here. Collagen bundles run in preferred directions that form the skin tension (cleavage) lines, so an incision along them gapes less and leaves a finer scar. The hypodermis is not part of the skin proper; it is loose connective tissue and fat lobules divided by fibrous septa that insulate, store energy, cushion, and carry the larger vessels and nerves."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers beneath the epidermis","cols":["Layer","Connective tissue","Vessels","Receptors and contents"],"rows":[["Papillary dermis","Loose; fine type III and I collagen, thin elastic fibers","Capillary loops in papillae; superficial (subpapillary) plexus at its base","Meissner corpuscles in glabrous skin; free nerve endings"],["Reticular dermis","Dense irregular; coarse type I collagen, coarse elastic fibers","Deep plexus at the dermis-hypodermis border","Follicle bases, glands, Ruffini endings, some Pacinian corpuscles"],["Hypodermis","Loose tissue with fat lobules and fibrous septa","Larger arteries and veins","Pacinian corpuscles; deep coils of some sweat glands"]],"caption":"Two horizontal vascular plexuses, connected by vertical vessels, supply the dermis; the epidermis itself has no vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Arteriovenous anastomoses, most numerous in the fingers, toes, and ears, let sympathetic nerves divert blood toward or away from the surface plexus, which is a major route of heat loss. Receptor depth follows function. Meissner corpuscles sit just under the epidermis and adapt quickly, signaling light touch and low-frequency flutter. Pacinian corpuscles lie deep, and their onion-like capsule filters out steady pressure so they respond only to rapid change and high-frequency vibration. Free nerve endings of thin A delta and C fibers extend into the epidermis and signal pain, temperature, and itch."},{"type":"table","title":"Cutaneous receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Adaptation","Main modality","Afferent fiber"],"rows":[["Meissner corpuscle","Dermal papillae of glabrous skin","Rapid","Light touch, low-frequency vibration","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Merkel cell-neurite complex","Stratum basale","Slow","Sustained pressure, texture, edges","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Ruffini ending","Reticular dermis","Slow","Skin stretch","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Pacinian corpuscle","Deep dermis and hypodermis","Very rapid","High-frequency vibration, rapid pressure change","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Free nerve ending","Dermis, extending into epidermis","Mostly slow","Pain, temperature, itch","Thin myelinated (A delta) and unmyelinated (C)"]],"caption":"Superficial receptors give fine spatial detail; deep receptors sense vibration transmitted through tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Each layer has its own disorders. Chronic sun exposure degrades papillary dermal collagen and deposits abnormal elastic material (solar elastosis), producing wrinkles. Inherited collagen defects such as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome make the dermis hyperextensible and fragile. Inflammation centered in the hypodermal septa, as in erythema nodosum, produces tender nodules rather than surface change."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C06","obj":"IN-1.6","title":"Eccrine, apocrine, and sebaceous glands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin glands are separated by three questions: where they open, how they release product, and what controls them. Eccrine glands open directly onto the surface; apocrine and sebaceous glands usually open into hair follicles, so neither is found on the palms and soles."},{"type":"table","title":"Skin glands compared","cols":["Gland","Location and opening","Secretion","Control","Linked disorder"],"rows":[["Eccrine","Nearly all skin, densest on palms, soles, forehead; opens on surface","Watery sweat; merocrine","Sympathetic cholinergic fibers on muscarinic (M3) receptors","Hyperhidrosis; salty sweat in cystic fibrosis"],["Apocrine","Axilla, areola, anogenital skin; opens into follicle","Viscous, odorless until skin bacteria act on it","Mainly adrenergic; active after puberty","Body odor"],["Sebaceous","All hair-bearing skin; opens into follicle; none on palms and soles","Sebum; holocrine","Androgens","Acne"]],"caption":"Modified apocrine glands include ceruminous glands of the ear canal and glands of Moll in the eyelid; meibomian glands are modified sebaceous glands that open directly on the lid margin."},{"type":"p","text":"Eccrine glands are the main tool of evaporative cooling. A coiled secretory portion in the deep dermis makes a fluid close to plasma in sodium and chloride, and the duct then reabsorbs sodium through ENaC and chloride through CFTR, so normal sweat reaching the surface is hypotonic. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR blocks ductal chloride reabsorption, which is why sweat chloride is high (about 60 mmol/L or more supports the diagnosis). These glands break the usual sympathetic rule: postganglionic sympathetic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. Hyperhidrosis is sweating beyond thermal need; the primary focal form affects palms, soles, and axillae through excess sympathetic drive to normal glands, while secondary generalized forms follow fever, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, or pheochromocytoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Apocrine glands have a wide lumen and empty into the follicle above the sebaceous duct. They enlarge at puberty and respond mainly to adrenergic stimulation such as emotional stress. Sebaceous glands release sebum (triglycerides, wax esters, squalene) by holocrine secretion: basal cells divide, fill with lipid, die, and break apart. Androgens, largely converted to dihydrotestosterone within the gland, enlarge sebaceous glands and increase output, which explains their activity at birth (maternal hormones) and puberty."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of acne","steps":["Androgens enlarge sebaceous glands and increase sebum output","Abnormal keratinization plugs the follicle, forming a microcomedo","Cutibacterium acnes multiplies in the lipid-rich, low-oxygen plug","Bacterial lipases and TLR2 signaling trigger inflammation","Follicle wall ruptures into dermis, forming papules, pustules, nodules"],"caption":"Acne is a disease of the pilosebaceous unit, so it spares the palms and soles, which have no sebaceous glands."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A person taking a drug with strong antimuscarinic action exercises in hot weather. What happens to sweating and body temperature?","explain":"Eccrine glands are driven by acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors even though the fibers are sympathetic, so sweating stops. Without evaporative cooling, heat is retained and core temperature rises, and the skin is hot, dry, and flushed. Apocrine secretion, which is mainly adrenergic, is little affected and contributes almost nothing to cooling.","check":"The control column of the gland table and the eccrine paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C07","obj":"IN-1.7","title":"Hair follicle, hair cycle, and nail unit","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A hair follicle is a downgrowth of epidermis into the dermis. Its deepest part, the bulb, holds matrix cells that divide rapidly around the dermal papilla, a cluster of specialized fibroblasts and capillaries whose signals start growth and set hair size. Matrix cells form the hair shaft and the inner root sheath, and bulb melanocytes color the shaft. The outer root sheath is continuous with the epidermis. Where the arrector pili muscle inserts, the outer root sheath forms the bulge, which holds multipotent stem cells that rebuild the lower follicle at each new cycle and help repair the epidermis after wounds."},{"type":"p","text":"The arrector pili is smooth muscle running from the bulge to the papillary dermis. Sympathetic alpha1-adrenergic stimulation contracts it, raising the hair and pressing on the sebaceous gland. Diseases that destroy the bulge cause scarring, permanent alopecia, while telogen effluvium, alopecia areata, and androgenetic alopecia spare the bulge and allow regrowth."},{"type":"table","title":"The scalp hair cycle","cols":["Phase","Duration","Share of scalp hairs","What happens"],"rows":[["Anagen","About 2 to 7 years","About 85 to 90%","Matrix cells divide and the shaft lengthens; duration sets maximum length"],["Catagen","About 2 to 3 weeks","About 1%","Matrix cells undergo apoptosis; lower follicle regresses toward the bulge"],["Telogen","About 3 months","About 10 to 15%","Club hair rests, then is shed as a new anagen hair grows beneath it"]],"caption":"Shedding about 50 to 100 scalp hairs a day is normal because follicles cycle out of step with each other."},{"type":"flow","title":"Telogen effluvium","steps":["Stressor: high fever, childbirth, surgery, severe illness, crash diet","Many anagen follicles enter catagen early and together","Follicles rest in telogen for about 3 months","New anagen hairs push out the club hairs","Diffuse shedding begins 2 to 3 months after the stressor"],"caption":"The bulge is spared, so the loss is nonscarring and hair regrows over months once the stressor is gone."},{"type":"example","title":"Using timing to name the hair loss","text":"A woman notices diffuse shedding of whole hairs with small white bulbs 3 months after delivering a baby. Another patient loses most of her scalp hair 2 weeks after starting chemotherapy. Work from the cycle. Club hairs that fall about 3 months after a stressor are telogen hairs, so the first patient has telogen effluvium. Chemotherapy halts mitosis in matrix cells of growing anagen follicles, so the shaft narrows and breaks within days to weeks, which is anagen effluvium. The delay is the length of telogen; the speed is the dependence of anagen on dividing cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The nail plate is hard keratin made by the nail matrix, which lies under the proximal nail fold; the pale lunula is the visible distal edge of the matrix. The plate slides forward over the nail bed, whose vascular dermis makes the nail look pink, and its free edge overhangs the hyponychium. The cuticle, made by the proximal fold (eponychium), seals the plate, and lateral folds frame its sides. Fingernails grow about 3 mm per month and toenails about 1 mm per month. Matrix injury causes permanent deformity, and a brief arrest of matrix growth during severe illness leaves a transverse groove (Beau line), the nail counterpart of telogen effluvium."},{"type":"p","text":"An ingrown nail usually involves the great toe. When the lateral edge of the plate is cut short and curved, or compressed by tight footwear, it grows into the soft tissue of the lateral nail fold. The plate then acts as a foreign body, provoking inflammation, granulation tissue, and often bacterial infection of the fold (paronychia)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C08","obj":"IN-1.8","title":"Histologic terms in skin pathology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dermatopathology terms name the abnormal layer or process, so each points to a mechanism: a layer too thick, a maturation step skipped, or cell connections failing."},{"type":"table","title":"Six core terms","cols":["Term","Meaning","Mechanism","Example diseases"],"rows":[["Hyperkeratosis","Thickened stratum corneum","More corneocytes made, or normal ones shed too slowly","Callus, ichthyosis vulgaris, psoriasis"],["Parakeratosis","Nuclei retained in the stratum corneum","Maturation so fast that nuclei are not destroyed","Psoriasis, actinic keratosis"],["Acanthosis","Thickened stratum spinosum (epidermal hyperplasia)","Increased keratinocyte proliferation","Psoriasis, seborrheic keratosis, chronic eczema"],["Spongiosis","Fluid between keratinocytes that remain attached","Intercellular edema from epidermal inflammation","Atopic and allergic contact dermatitis"],["Acantholysis","Keratinocytes lose attachment and separate","Desmosome failure, usually from anti-desmoglein antibodies","Pemphigus vulgaris and foliaceus"],["Dyskeratosis","Single keratinocytes keratinize early and abnormally","Disordered maturation of individual cells","Actinic keratosis, squamous cell carcinoma, Darier disease"]],"caption":"Two pairs are often confused: hyperkeratosis (corneum) versus acanthosis (spinosum), and spongiosis (edema, cells attached) versus acantholysis (cells detached)."},{"type":"p","text":"Psoriasis shows how several terms arise from one mechanism. Dendritic cells release IL-23, which sustains Th17 cells, and their IL-17 and IL-22 drive keratinocyte proliferation. Transit through the epidermis falls from about 4 weeks to a few days, so the spinous layer thickens with long, regular rete ridges (acanthosis), cells reach the surface without passing through a normal granular layer, and nuclei are retained (parakeratosis) in a thick scale. Neutrophils collect in the corneum as Munro microabscesses. Capillaries in the papillae dilate while the epidermis over them thins, so lifting a scale causes pinpoint bleeding (the Auspitz sign)."},{"type":"p","text":"Eczematous dermatitis is the prototype of spongiosis. Inflammation, from Th2 responses in atopic dermatitis or hapten-specific T cells in allergic contact dermatitis, lets fluid enter the epidermis and push keratinocytes apart while their desmosomes hold, so the junctions appear prominent. Severe spongiosis forms small intraepidermal vesicles, and chronic scratching adds acanthosis and hyperkeratosis (lichenification). Acantholysis differs because adhesion itself is lost: in pemphigus, detached keratinocytes round up and float freely in the blister. Dyskeratosis signals disordered maturation of individual cells and, with atypia, is a feature of dysplasia and squamous cell carcinoma, where keratin pearls form."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin structure ties the Part I disciplines together through one idea: each level is held by specific proteins, and failure of a protein produces a predictable lesion. Embryology supplies the cells, with ectoderm forming keratinocytes, neural crest forming melanocytes, mesoderm forming the dermis, and hematopoietic precursors supplying Langerhans cells. Biochemistry and genetics explain inherited fragility (keratins, laminin 332, collagens XVII and VII), barrier failure (filaggrin), and pigment disorders (tyrosinase). Immunology explains the acquired forms, with type II autoantibodies against desmogleins and hemidesmosome proteins and type IV sensitization through Langerhans cells. Microbiology appears where bacterial toxins cleave desmoglein 1 and where Cutibacterium acnes inflames a plugged follicle."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology uses the same map in reverse. A biopsy reports a split level and a histologic term, and immunofluorescence reports the pattern of antibody, and from these the target protein can be inferred. The limits are real: clinical blister type overlaps, early lesions may lack the classic picture, and some diseases involve more than one antigen, so split level, immunofluorescence, and the clinical setting are read together rather than relying on any single finding."}],"summary":["Keratinocytes mature from basale to corneum over about 4 weeks, switching from keratins 5 and 14 to 1 and 10; stratum lucidum appears only in thick skin of the palms and soles.","The barrier forms in the granulosum: filaggrin bundles keratin, lamellar bodies secrete lipid, and transglutaminase builds the cornified envelope.","Melanocytes (neural crest, basal layer) transfer melanosomes to keratinocytes; skin color reflects melanosome activity, not melanocyte number.","Langerhans cells (CD1a, langerin, Birbeck granules) carry antigen to lymph nodes; Merkel cells are slowly adapting touch receptors in the basal layer.","Anti-desmoglein antibodies split the epidermis from within (pemphigus, flaccid blisters, Nikolsky positive); anti-BP180 and BP230 antibodies lift it off the basement membrane (bullous pemphigoid, tense blisters).","Split level predicts the protein: keratin 5 or 14 within basal cells, laminin 332 or collagen XVII in the lamina lucida, type VII collagen below the lamina densa with scarring and milia.","The papillary dermis is loose with capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles; the reticular dermis is dense type I collagen; Pacinian corpuscles lie deep.","Eccrine glands are sympathetic but muscarinic and open on the surface; apocrine and sebaceous glands open into follicles, and androgen-driven sebum underlies acne.","Telogen effluvium appears about 2 to 3 months after a stressor because telogen lasts about 3 months; bulge destruction causes permanent, scarring alopecia.","Hyperkeratosis and acanthosis describe thick corneum and spinosum; parakeratosis means retained nuclei; spongiosis is edema with cells attached, acantholysis is cells detached."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Dendritic cells and antigen presentation; hypersensitivity diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic sensations, tactile receptors; body temperature regulation and sweating"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Amino acid conversion to specialized products, melanin synthesis"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary system"}]},"IN-2":{"unit":"IN-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01","title":"Ectoderm Embryology and Skin Development","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the ectoderm as it splits into surface ectoderm, neuroectoderm, and neural crest, then traces how the skin, its appendages, and its pigment cells form before birth. Board questions usually name a structure and ask for its germ layer, or describe a newborn skin finding or pigment disorder and ask where development went wrong. The unifying idea is that melanocytes are migrants: they start at the neural tube and must travel, survive, and then make melanin. Knowing which of those steps failed explains a blue sacral patch, a white forelock, and generalized albinism.","prerequisites":["The three germ layers and gastrulation","Somite organization into sclerotome and dermomyotome","Epidermal layers, melanocytes, and adnexa of adult skin (IN-1)","Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling","Autosomal dominant and recessive inheritance"],"connections":[{"to":"Neural tube embryology (NE-1)","how":"Neurulation creates the neural tube and releases the neural crest, so neural tube and crest defects share their origin in the same event."},{"to":"Skin layers and adnexa (IN-1)","how":"The adult epidermis, follicle, and glands described there are built by the developmental steps in this chapter."},{"to":"Pigmentation changes of the skin (IN-13-A)","how":"Vitiligo, albinism, and nevi are classified by whether melanocytes are missing, present but inactive, or proliferating."},{"to":"Endocrine embryology (EN-1)","how":"The anterior and posterior pituitary, adrenal medulla, and thyroid C cells come from three different ectodermal lineages."},{"to":"Congenital gastrointestinal disease (GI-13-H)","how":"Hirschsprung disease is a failure of neural crest migration to the distal bowel, the enteric counterpart of piebaldism."}],"terms":[{"term":"Surface ectoderm","def":"Ectoderm that remains outside the neural plate and forms the epidermis, its appendages, and several sensory and oral epithelia."},{"term":"Neuroectoderm","def":"Ectoderm of the neural plate that folds into the neural tube and forms the central nervous system, retina, and posterior pituitary."},{"term":"Neural crest","def":"Cells from the border of the neural plate that undergo an epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition and migrate to form many peripheral tissues."},{"term":"Epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition","def":"The loss of cell junctions and polarity that lets an epithelial cell become a motile mesenchymal cell."},{"term":"Dermomyotome","def":"The dorsal part of a somite, which gives rise to the dermis of the back (dermatome) and to skeletal muscle (myotome)."},{"term":"Periderm","def":"A temporary superficial layer of flattened fetal epidermal cells that covers the developing epidermis and is later shed."},{"term":"Melanoblast","def":"A neural crest-derived precursor of the melanocyte that is still migrating and does not yet make melanin."},{"term":"Dermal papilla","def":"A condensation of mesenchyme at the base of the hair bud that signals to the epithelium to form and grow the hair."},{"term":"Lanugo","def":"Fine, unpigmented first fetal hair, appearing around the fifth month and mostly shed before or around birth."},{"term":"Vernix caseosa","def":"A whitish fetal skin coating of sebum, shed periderm and epidermal cells, and water that protects the skin in amniotic fluid."},{"term":"Tyndall effect","def":"Scattering of shorter blue wavelengths by tissue, which makes melanin deep in the dermis appear blue-gray instead of brown."},{"term":"KIT","def":"The receptor tyrosine kinase for stem cell factor, required for melanoblast survival, proliferation, and migration."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C01","obj":"IN-2.1","title":"Surface ectoderm, neuroectoderm, and neural crest","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"After gastrulation, the ectoderm is a single sheet on the dorsal surface of the embryo. Around day 18 to 19, the notochord and prechordal mesoderm secrete noggin, chordin, and follistatin, which block bone morphogenetic protein 4 (BMP4) in the overlying ectoderm. Ectoderm with low BMP signaling thickens into the neural plate (neuroectoderm); ectoderm that keeps high BMP signaling becomes surface ectoderm. The neural plate folds, and its edges fuse in the midline starting in the future cervical region, forming the neural tube."},{"type":"p","text":"Cells at the border between the plate and surface ectoderm receive intermediate BMP signals together with Wnt and fibroblast growth factor signals. They express transcription factors such as SNAI2 (Slug), SOX10, and PAX3, lose their epithelial junctions, and migrate away as neural crest. Cranial crest begins migrating before the folds fuse; trunk crest leaves as the tube closes. Because all three lineages arise from one sheet in one event, a single developmental error can affect the brain, the skin, and crest derivatives together."},{"type":"flow","title":"One ectoderm, three lineages","steps":["Notochord secretes noggin and chordin","BMP4 blocked in midline ectoderm","Neural plate forms and folds","Folds fuse into the neural tube (neuroectoderm)","Border cells lose their junctions and leave as crest","Lateral ectoderm stays as surface ectoderm"],"caption":"Low BMP gives neural plate, high BMP gives surface ectoderm, and the border between them gives neural crest."},{"type":"table","title":"Major ectodermal derivatives","cols":["Lineage","Skin and appendages","Head and sense organs","Other"],"rows":[["Surface ectoderm","Epidermis, hair, nails, sweat and sebaceous glands, mammary glands","Lens, corneal epithelium, inner ear epithelium (otic placode), olfactory epithelium","Anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), tooth enamel (ameloblasts), oral epithelium anterior to the oropharyngeal membrane"],["Neuroectoderm","None","Retina including pigment epithelium, optic nerve, iris sphincter and dilator muscles","CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependyma, posterior pituitary, pineal gland"],["Neural crest","Melanocytes, dermis of the face","Corneal stroma and endothelium, facial bones and cartilage, pia and arachnoid over the forebrain","Peripheral ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, odontoblasts, thyroid C cells"]],"caption":"Microglia are the exception in the CNS: they come from yolk sac macrophage precursors (mesoderm), not neuroectoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"Several pairs of adjacent structures have different origins. The anterior pituitary grows up from the roof of the primitive mouth (Rathke pouch, surface ectoderm), while the posterior pituitary grows down from the diencephalon (neuroectoderm); remnants of Rathke pouch give rise to craniopharyngioma. In the eye, the lens and corneal epithelium are surface ectoderm, but the retina and its pigment epithelium are outgrowths of the brain. In the tooth, enamel is surface ectoderm while dentin is made by crest-derived odontoblasts."},{"type":"p","text":"Surface ectoderm also forms placodes, local thickenings that give rise to the lens, the otic vesicle (inner ear membranous labyrinth), the olfactory epithelium, and some sensory neurons of cranial nerve ganglia V, VII, IX, and X, which share these ganglia with neural crest. Mammary glands bud from paired mammary ridges (milk lines) of surface ectoderm running from axilla to groin; extra nipples (polythelia) appear along this line. Skin appendages arise from shared placode signaling, which is why X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (EDA gene) causes sparse hair, absent sweat glands with heat intolerance, and missing or conical teeth together."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has a lens defect and a pituitary tumor arising from the sella that contains calcifications. Which shared germ layer links the lens and this tumor?","explain":"Surface ectoderm. The lens forms from the lens placode, and craniopharyngioma arises from remnants of Rathke pouch, an upgrowth of oral surface ectoderm. The posterior pituitary and retina, by contrast, are neuroectoderm.","check":"The derivatives table and the paragraph on adjacent structures with different origins."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C02","obj":"IN-2.2","title":"Neural crest derivatives and the regional origin of the dermis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural crest cells are multipotent, and their fate depends largely on the route they take and the signals they meet. In the trunk, crest cells follow two main routes. The ventral pathway runs through the rostral half of each sclerotome; cells on it become dorsal root ganglia, sympathetic chain ganglia, Schwann cells, and chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. The dorsolateral pathway runs between the dermomyotome and the surface ectoderm; cells on it become melanoblasts. Vagal and sacral crest colonize the gut wall as enteric ganglia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Trunk neural crest routes","steps":["Crest cells leave the dorsal neural tube","Ventral route through rostral sclerotome","Dorsal root and sympathetic ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla","Dorsolateral route under the surface ectoderm","Melanoblasts enter dermis, then epidermis and hair bulbs"],"caption":"The route determines the fate: ventral cells form neurons, glia, and chromaffin cells, and dorsolateral cells form pigment cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Neural crest derivatives and the disorders they explain","cols":["Derivative","Function","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Melanocytes","Melanin synthesis in skin, hair, uvea, and inner ear (stria vascularis)","Piebaldism, Waardenburg syndrome, melanoma"],["Schwann cells","Myelin and support for peripheral axons","Schwannoma, neurofibroma"],["Dorsal root and autonomic ganglia","Sensory and autonomic neurons","Neuroblastoma arises in the sympathetic lineage"],["Enteric ganglia","Myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Hirschsprung disease"],["Adrenal medulla chromaffin cells","Catecholamine secretion","Pheochromocytoma, neuroblastoma"],["Odontoblasts","Dentin of the tooth","Dentin is crest-derived even though the enamel over it is surface ectoderm"],["Cranial crest mesenchyme","Facial bones, cartilage, and dermis; pia and arachnoid over the forebrain; thyroid C cells","Treacher Collins syndrome, medullary thyroid carcinoma (C cells)"]],"caption":"Tumors and congenital defects of these tissues are grouped as neurocristopathies."},{"type":"h","text":"Why facial dermis is neural crest and back dermis is somitic"},{"type":"p","text":"The epidermis is surface ectoderm everywhere, but the dermis forms from whatever mesenchyme lies beneath it, and that mesenchyme differs by region. Somites begin in the occipital region, so there are no somites under the face. Instead, cranial neural crest streams into the frontonasal process and pharyngeal arches and forms most of the facial mesenchyme, which becomes facial bone, cartilage, connective tissue, and dermis. Over the back, each somite's dermomyotome releases dermatome cells that spread beneath the overlying ectoderm and form the dorsal dermis. In the limbs and ventral body wall, the dermis comes from the somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm."},{"type":"table","title":"Origin of the two skin layers by region","cols":["Region","Epidermis","Dermis"],"rows":[["Face","Surface ectoderm","Cranial neural crest"],["Back","Surface ectoderm","Paraxial mesoderm (somite dermatomes)"],["Limbs and ventral body wall","Surface ectoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm (somatic layer)"]],"caption":"In every region, the epidermis and dermis of the same patch of skin can come from different germ layers."},{"type":"p","text":"Dermal mesenchyme signals to the overlying epidermis and helps decide what kind of appendages form, so the dermis shapes regional skin character. Do not confuse the embryonic dermatome (the part of a somite that forms dorsal dermis) with the clinical dermatome (the skin area supplied by one spinal nerve): limb skin has a segmental nerve supply even though its dermis is lateral plate mesoderm."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out the origin of one patch of skin","text":"Take a biopsy from the cheek and one from the skin over the lower thoracic spine. Both epidermal layers are surface ectoderm, and the melanocytes in both are neural crest. The difference lies in the dermis. The cheek lies over the first pharyngeal arch, which has no somites beneath it and is filled with cranial crest mesenchyme, so its dermis is neural crest. The back lies directly over the somites, so its dermis comes from somite dermatomes, which is paraxial mesoderm. A forearm biopsy would differ again, since limb buds are built from lateral plate mesoderm."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C03","obj":"IN-2.3","title":"Fetal skin development","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fetal skin turns a single sheet of surface ectoderm into a stratified, keratinized barrier in a fixed order. During the second month, that single layer divides into a deep basal (germinative) layer and a superficial periderm of flattened cells, which seals the embryo while the layers beneath it mature. Proliferation of the basal layer then adds an intermediate layer, and by about the end of the fourth month the epidermis has its definitive arrangement of basal, spinous, granular, and cornified layers. As keratinization proceeds, periderm cells are shed into the amniotic fluid."},{"type":"p","text":"The barrier is not mature until late gestation. The stratum corneum becomes an effective barrier at about 32 to 34 weeks, so a very preterm infant has thin, permeable skin that loses water and heat quickly and absorbs substances applied to it more readily."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate timeline of fetal skin development","cols":["Time","Event"],"rows":[["Second month (weeks 5 to 8)","Single ectodermal layer divides into basal layer and periderm"],["First trimester","Melanoblasts migrate from the neural crest into the dermis and then the epidermis"],["About weeks 9 to 12","Hair buds grow down into the dermis; dermal papillae form at their tips"],["By about week 16","Epidermis has its adult layers; periderm is being shed"],["About weeks 17 to 20","Lanugo covers the body, after the first hairs appear on brow and upper lip near the end of month 3; vernix caseosa begins to accumulate"],["About weeks 32 to 34","Stratum corneum barrier becomes functional"],["Around birth","Most lanugo has been shed; vernix covers the term infant"]],"caption":"Timings are approximate and vary slightly between embryology texts."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanoblasts from the neural crest migrate under the surface ectoderm, cross the dermis, and enter the epidermis during the first trimester, where they settle among basal cells and in developing hair bulbs. They begin producing melanin in fetal life, but skin color continues to darken after birth as melanin production increases."},{"type":"p","text":"Hair forms through reciprocal signaling between epithelium and mesenchyme. Basal cells thicken into a placode, which grows down into the dermis as a hair bud. Dermal mesenchyme condenses at the tip of the bud as the dermal papilla, and the bud end invaginates around it to form the bulb. The papilla then signals the surrounding matrix cells to divide and produce the hair shaft. Sebaceous glands bud from the side of the follicle wall, and the bulge forms below them, where the arrector pili muscle (from mesenchyme) attaches. The first hairs, lanugo, are fine and unpigmented; most are shed into the amniotic fluid late in gestation and are replaced by vellus and terminal hairs. Preterm infants are often still covered in lanugo."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hair follicle formation","steps":["Basal cells thicken into a hair placode","Hair bud grows down into the dermis","Mesenchyme condenses at the tip as the dermal papilla","Bud end invaginates around the papilla to form the bulb","Matrix cells divide and form the hair shaft","Sebaceous gland buds from the follicle wall"],"caption":"The dermal papilla instructs the epithelium; without it, no hair shaft forms."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal sebaceous glands are active in late gestation under androgen stimulation. Their sebum mixes with shed periderm and epidermal cells, water, and lanugo to form vernix caseosa, a whitish coat that protects the skin from maceration during months in amniotic fluid. Vernix also contains antimicrobial proteins and helps moisturize the skin after birth. A post-term infant often has little vernix left and shows dry, peeling skin, while a preterm infant may have vernix but an immature barrier beneath it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C04","obj":"IN-2.4","title":"Dermal melanocytosis and other newborn skin lesions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanoblasts reach the epidermis by crossing the dermis, and almost all dermal melanocytes normally disappear or move on by birth. Some persist, most often over the lumbosacral region and buttocks. These arrested dermal melanocytes are spindle-shaped, lie deep in the reticular dermis, and keep making melanin. The result is dermal melanocytosis (Mongolian spot): a flat, uniform, blue-gray patch present at birth. It is common in infants of Asian, African, Native American, and Hispanic descent and uncommon in those of European descent. Most patches fade during early childhood, though those outside the sacral area more often persist."},{"type":"p","text":"The color is explained by depth. Epidermal melanin absorbs most visible light and looks brown, while deep dermal melanin is reached mainly by longer wavelengths and the shorter blue wavelengths scatter back from the tissue above it (the Tyndall effect), so the patch looks blue-gray. The same principle colors blue nevi. Because a sacral or extensive patch can be mistaken for bruising in suspected child abuse, documenting it at birth matters. Unlike a bruise, it does not change color over days and is not tender."},{"type":"flow","title":"From arrested migration to a blue-gray patch","steps":["Melanoblasts cross the dermis toward the epidermis","Some remain in the deep dermis, usually sacral","Dermal melanocytes keep producing melanin","Overlying tissue scatters blue light back (Tyndall effect)","Flat blue-gray patch present at birth","Most fade during early childhood"],"caption":"The lesion is benign; its color comes from the depth of the pigment, not from a different pigment."},{"type":"p","text":"Three other common newborn lesions are separated by the cell involved and by their course. A congenital melanocytic nevus is a proliferation of nevus cells present from fetal life, often reaching deep into the dermis and around adnexa, so it is brown and may grow coarse hair; large and giant lesions carry increased melanoma risk. An infantile hemangioma is a benign proliferation of GLUT1-positive endothelium, so it is absent or faint at birth, grows rapidly for months, then involutes over years. A port-wine stain is a capillary malformation of mature, dilated dermal capillaries and venules, usually from a somatic GNAQ mutation, so it is present at birth, grows only in proportion to the child, never regresses, and tends to darken and thicken in adulthood. Unilateral involvement of the ophthalmic (V1) distribution can accompany leptomeningeal and ocular vascular malformation (Sturge-Weber syndrome), with seizures and glaucoma. Nevus simplex, the far more common faint midline patch on glabella, eyelids, or nape, fades over the first years and carries no such risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing pigmented and vascular lesions at birth","cols":["Lesion","Cells involved","At birth","Course"],"rows":[["Dermal melanocytosis","Arrested melanocytes in the deep dermis","Flat blue-gray patch, usually lumbosacral","Usually fades in early childhood"],["Congenital melanocytic nevus","Nevus cells in dermis and around adnexa","Brown, sometimes hairy, may be raised","Grows with the child; melanoma risk mainly in large lesions"],["Infantile hemangioma","Proliferating GLUT1-positive endothelium","Absent or faint mark","Rapid growth over months, then slow involution"],["Port-wine stain","Mature dilated capillaries (GNAQ)","Flat pink to red patch","Persists, darkens, may thicken; V1 suggests Sturge-Weber"]],"caption":"A tumor grows faster than the child and may regress; a malformation grows in proportion and persists."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has a unilateral red patch covering one side of the forehead and the upper eyelid, fully formed at birth and grown only in proportion at 6 months. A second infant has no lesion at birth but a rapidly enlarging red nodule at 6 weeks. Which lesion will regress?","explain":"The second. A lesion that appears after birth and grows faster than the child is an infantile hemangioma, a proliferating endothelial tumor that later involutes. The first is a port-wine stain, a capillary malformation that never regresses, and its V1 location raises concern for Sturge-Weber syndrome.","check":"The newborn lesion table and the tumor versus malformation distinction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C05","obj":"IN-2.5","title":"Piebaldism versus albinism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A white patch of skin can arise in three ways: melanocytes never arrived (piebaldism), they arrived but cannot make melanin (albinism), or they were destroyed after arriving (vitiligo)."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanoblasts depend on the KIT receptor tyrosine kinase. Its ligand, stem cell factor, is produced along their migratory route, and KIT signaling keeps melanoblasts alive and drives their proliferation and migration. Piebaldism is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a loss-of-function KIT mutation. With reduced signaling, melanoblasts fail to reach the sites farthest from their dorsal origin or to survive there. Those sites lack melanocytes permanently, giving a white forelock and white patches on the central forehead, ventral trunk, and mid-arms and legs, usually sparing the back, hands, and feet. The patches are stable for life and often contain islands of normal or darker pigment. Biopsy shows absent or nearly absent melanocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Oculocutaneous albinism is autosomal recessive and affects pigment synthesis, not migration. Melanocyte number and distribution are normal, but melanin output is reduced. In type 1, tyrosinase, the copper-dependent enzyme that converts tyrosine to DOPA and DOPA to dopaquinone, is absent or deficient. Other types involve proteins needed for melanosome function, such as the OCA2 (P) protein. Because every melanocyte is affected, hypopigmentation involves all skin, hair, and eyes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Melanin synthesis and where albinism fails","steps":["Tyrosine enters the melanosome","Tyrosinase converts tyrosine to DOPA","Tyrosinase converts DOPA to dopaquinone","Dopaquinone forms eumelanin or pheomelanin","Melanosomes transfer to keratinocytes"],"caption":"Type 1 albinism blocks the first two steps; the melanocytes are present but produce little or no melanin."},{"type":"p","text":"The eye findings show where pigment matters for development. The retinal pigment epithelium is neuroectoderm, not neural crest, but it uses tyrosinase too, so albinism reduces its pigment. Melanin is needed for normal foveal development and for routing of optic nerve fibers, so albinism causes foveal hypoplasia, excessive crossing of fibers at the optic chiasm, nystagmus, reduced acuity, and photophobia with iris transillumination. Lacking melanin, the skin has little protection from ultraviolet light and develops sun damage and carcinomas, especially squamous cell carcinoma. In piebaldism the eyes are normal, because the retinal pigment epithelium does not depend on crest migration. When a crest disorder does affect the eye and ear, as in Waardenburg syndrome (PAX3, MITF, SOX10), iris heterochromia and deafness appear, since melanocytes of the iris stroma and cochlear stria vascularis are crest-derived."},{"type":"table","title":"White skin: absent melanocytes or absent melanin","cols":["Feature","Piebaldism","Oculocutaneous albinism","Vitiligo"],"rows":[["Defect","KIT; failed melanoblast migration or survival","Tyrosinase or melanosome protein; failed melanin synthesis","Acquired autoimmune destruction of melanocytes"],["Inheritance","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","Not Mendelian; polygenic susceptibility"],["Melanocytes in white skin","Absent","Normal number","Absent"],["Onset and pattern","Birth; forelock, ventral and limb patches; stable","Birth; generalized skin, hair, eyes","Acquired; often periorificial and acral; may spread"],["Eyes","Normal","Nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia, low acuity","Usually normal"]],"caption":"Count the melanocytes first, then ask whether the defect is present at birth and whether it is localized or generalized."},{"type":"example","title":"Two pale children","text":"Child A has a white forelock and white patches on the abdomen and knees since birth, with normal eyes; a parent has the same forelock. Child B has white hair, very pale skin everywhere, and nystagmus; both parents are normally pigmented. In A, the patchy, stable pattern and dominant inheritance point to piebaldism, and a biopsy of a white patch would show no melanocytes, because melanoblasts never colonized those ventral and distal sites. In B, the generalized pattern, eye findings, and unaffected carrier parents point to recessive albinism. A biopsy would show melanocytes in normal numbers that make little melanin, and in type 1 the defect is tyrosinase."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and genetics explain the patterns. The ectoderm splits into three lineages in one event, so the same signaling defect can involve skin, nervous system, and crest derivatives, as in ectodermal dysplasias and Waardenburg syndrome. Neural crest cells share migration machinery, which is why defects in genes such as SOX10 or EDNRB can produce pigment loss, deafness, and Hirschsprung disease together, and why piebaldism and Hirschsprung disease are both failures of crest colonization. Regional dermal origin (crest, somite, or lateral plate) explains why facial anomalies follow pharyngeal arch boundaries and why dorsal dermis is organized segmentally."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pathology explain the pigment findings. Melanin synthesis begins at tyrosinase, so tyrosinase loss gives albinism, and in untreated phenylketonuria, low tyrosine and high phenylalanine reduce melanin and lighten hair and skin. Histology answers most pigment questions: absent melanocytes (piebaldism, vitiligo), normal melanocytes with little pigment (albinism), or melanocytes in the wrong place (dermal melanocytosis). The limits are clinical: a Mongolian spot cannot be told from a bruise by color alone, and only the course over weeks separates an early hemangioma from a capillary malformation."}],"summary":["BMP inhibition from the notochord makes neural plate; high BMP leaves surface ectoderm; the border forms neural crest by epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition.","Surface ectoderm forms epidermis, hair, nails, cutaneous and mammary glands, lens, corneal epithelium, anterior pituitary, and enamel.","Neuroectoderm forms CNS neurons and macroglia, retina and pigment epithelium, posterior pituitary, and pineal gland; microglia are mesodermal.","Neural crest forms melanocytes, Schwann cells, peripheral ganglia, adrenal medulla, odontoblasts, and facial mesenchyme including facial dermis.","Dermis comes from cranial crest in the face, somite dermatomes in the back, and lateral plate mesoderm in the limbs and ventral body wall.","Fetal epidermis passes from one layer to basal layer plus periderm to adult layers by about the fourth month; the barrier matures at about 32 to 34 weeks.","The dermal papilla induces hair formation; lanugo appears around the fifth month, and vernix is sebum plus shed cells that protects skin in amniotic fluid.","Dermal melanocytosis is arrested dermal melanocytes that look blue-gray by the Tyndall effect and usually fade; it must not be mistaken for bruising.","Infantile hemangioma (GLUT1 tumor) appears after birth, grows, and involutes; port-wine stain (GNAQ malformation) is present at birth and persists.","Piebaldism (dominant KIT) lacks melanocytes in stable patches with normal eyes; albinism (recessive, tyrosinase in type 1) has normal melanocytes that make little melanin."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary system; ectodermal germ layer derivatives and neural crest cells"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes; blood vessels, vascular ectasias and hemangiomas"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Amino acid conversion to specialized products, melanin synthesis"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin, melanocytes and epidermal appendages"}]},"IN-3":{"unit":"IN-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01","title":"Membrane Transport and Membrane Potential","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the plasma membrane is built, how solutes and water cross it, and how ion gradients across it create the resting membrane potential. These ideas sit underneath nearly every system: cell swelling in hypoxic injury, glucose absorption and glucosuria, the effect of potassium on nerve, muscle, and the ECG, and the salty sweat and thick mucus of cystic fibrosis. Board questions usually change one variable, such as a pump, a channel, an ion concentration, or the solution around a cell, and ask you to predict cell volume, membrane potential, or excitability. If you can reason from gradients, permeability, and effective solute, you can answer them without memorizing each case.","prerequisites":["Structure of fatty acids (saturated and cis unsaturated), phospholipids, and cholesterol","Concentration units: mmol/L, mOsm/L, and mOsm/kg, and base 10 logarithms","Basic electricity: charge, voltage, current, and the idea of a driving force","ATP production by glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation","Histology of the eccrine sweat gland (IN-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cell adaptation and injury (IN-8)","how":"Failure of the Na+/K+-ATPase when ATP falls is the mechanism of cellular swelling, the first sign of reversible hypoxic injury."},{"to":"Skin layers and adnexa (IN-1)","how":"Eccrine duct reabsorption of NaCl through CFTR and ENaC explains why sweat is hypotonic and why it is salty in cystic fibrosis."},{"to":"Cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"Extracellular K+ and the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger link the resting potential and the Na+ gradient to cardiac excitability, ECG changes, and contractility."},{"to":"Renal reabsorption (UR-6)","how":"SGLT2 and SGLT1 in the proximal tubule are saturable carriers, so glucose appears in the urine once their transport maximum is exceeded."},{"to":"Digestion and absorption (GI-6)","how":"Enterocytes absorb glucose and galactose by Na+-coupled SGLT1 and release them by GLUT2, a model of secondary active transport."}],"terms":[{"term":"Amphipathic","def":"Having both a water-attracting (polar) region and a water-repelling (nonpolar) region, as phospholipids and cholesterol do."},{"term":"Glycocalyx","def":"The carbohydrate coat on the outer cell surface formed by the sugar chains of glycoproteins, glycolipids, and proteoglycans."},{"term":"Transport maximum (Tmax)","def":"The highest rate a carrier-mediated process can reach once every carrier is occupied, which makes the process saturable."},{"term":"Secondary active transport","def":"Uphill movement of one solute powered by the downhill movement of another ion, usually Na+, whose gradient was built by an ATPase."},{"term":"Electrogenic","def":"Describes a transporter that moves net charge across the membrane in each cycle and therefore directly changes membrane voltage."},{"term":"Osmolality","def":"The number of osmoles of all dissolved particles per kilogram of water; osmolarity is the same count per liter of solution."},{"term":"Tonicity","def":"The effect of a solution on cell volume, determined only by the solutes that cannot cross the cell membrane."},{"term":"Effective osmole","def":"A solute that stays on one side of the membrane (reflection coefficient near 1) and therefore exerts a lasting osmotic pull on water."},{"term":"Equilibrium (Nernst) potential","def":"The membrane voltage at which the electrical force on an ion exactly balances its concentration gradient, so its net flux is zero."},{"term":"Resting membrane potential","def":"The steady voltage of the cell interior relative to the outside in an unstimulated cell, set mainly by K+ permeability and the K+ gradient."},{"term":"Gibbs-Donnan effect","def":"The uneven distribution of diffusible ions and the extra osmotic load that result when impermeant charged molecules are trapped on one side of a membrane."},{"term":"Inactivation gate","def":"The part of a voltage-gated Na+ channel that closes during sustained depolarization and reopens only after the membrane repolarizes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C01","obj":"IN-3.1","title":"Plasma membrane components and membrane fluidity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The plasma membrane is a lipid bilayer about 7.5 to 10 nm thick with proteins set into it. Phospholipids are amphipathic: a polar head group attached to two fatty acid tails. In water they assemble spontaneously into a bilayer, heads facing the cytosol and the extracellular fluid and tails forming a hydrophobic core. That core is the barrier. Small nonpolar molecules such as O2, CO2, and steroids dissolve through it, small uncharged polar molecules cross slowly, and ions and larger polar molecules such as glucose essentially cannot cross without a protein."},{"type":"p","text":"The two leaflets differ. Phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin predominate in the outer leaflet, while phosphatidylserine, phosphatidylethanolamine, and phosphatidylinositol lie mainly in the inner leaflet, an asymmetry maintained by ATP-dependent flippases that return aminophospholipids to the inner leaflet. Phosphatidylserine exposed on the outer surface signals phagocytes to remove an apoptotic cell, and inner leaflet phosphatidylinositol, once phosphorylated to phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2), is the substrate that phospholipase C cleaves into the second messengers IP3 and diacylglycerol."},{"type":"p","text":"Integral proteins cross the bilayer, usually as alpha helices of about 20 hydrophobic amino acids, and can be removed only with detergents that dissolve the lipid; they form channels, carriers, pumps, receptors, and adhesion molecules. Peripheral proteins attach to lipid heads or integral proteins by ionic and hydrogen bonds; on the cytoplasmic side many link the membrane to the cytoskeleton, such as spectrin and ankyrin in red cells. The glycocalyx, built from the sugar chains of glycoproteins, glycolipids, and proteoglycans, lies only on the outer surface. Sialic acid and sulfated sugars give it a net negative charge, and its sugars serve as recognition and adhesion sites, including the ABO blood group antigens."},{"type":"p","text":"Fluidity describes how freely lipids and proteins move sideways within a leaflet; movement between leaflets is rare without enzymes. It depends on how tightly the tails pack. A cis double bond puts a fixed kink in a tail, so unsaturated tails cannot pack closely and fluidity rises. Shorter tails make fewer van der Waals contacts and also raise fluidity, while long saturated tails pack tightly and order the membrane. Cholesterol acts as a buffer rather than a one-way stiffener. Its rigid ring system lies beside the upper part of the tails: at body temperature it restrains tail motion, lowering fluidity and permeability to small water-soluble molecules, and at low temperature it wedges the tails apart and prevents them from freezing into a gel. Fluidity therefore stays nearly constant over a range of temperatures."},{"type":"table","title":"Plasma membrane components","cols":["Component","Structure","Main role","Exam link"],"rows":[["Phospholipids","Polar head, two fatty acid tails; asymmetric leaflets","Barrier to ions and polar solutes","Outer phosphatidylserine marks apoptotic cells"],["Cholesterol","Rigid steroid rings with a small polar hydroxyl group","Buffers fluidity; lowers permeability","Excess red cell membrane cholesterol in severe liver disease produces spur cells (acanthocytes)"],["Integral proteins","Span the bilayer; detergent needed to remove","Channels, carriers, pumps, receptors","Targets of most transport defects, such as CFTR (C07)"],["Peripheral proteins","Bound to the surface by ionic and hydrogen bonds","Cytoskeletal links, enzymes, signaling","Spectrin and ankyrin defects in hereditary spherocytosis"],["GPI-anchored proteins","Outer proteins tethered by a glycolipid anchor","Enzymes, receptors, complement regulators","Lost CD55 and CD59 in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"],["Glycocalyx","Sugar chains on outer glycoproteins, glycolipids, proteoglycans","Negative surface charge, recognition, adhesion","ABO blood group antigens"]],"caption":"Each component, its structure, and the clinical example most often used to test it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C02","obj":"IN-3.2","title":"Mechanisms of membrane transport","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Solutes cross the membrane through the lipid itself, through a channel, on a carrier, or inside a vesicle. Two questions classify every mechanism. Does the solute move down its electrochemical gradient (passive) or against it (active)? If active, is the energy taken directly from ATP or borrowed from another ion's gradient?"},{"type":"p","text":"Simple diffusion is net movement down a gradient without binding to a carrier. Its rate follows Fick's law: flux equals permeability times membrane area times the concentration difference, and permeability rises with lipid solubility and falls with molecular size and membrane thickness. Because no binding sites fill up, the rate rises in a straight line with the gradient and never saturates. O2, CO2, and lipid-soluble drugs cross this way. Diffusion through open ion channels, which are selective and gated by voltage, ligand, or stretch, is also unsaturated over the physiological range."},{"type":"p","text":"Facilitated diffusion uses a carrier that binds the solute on one side, changes shape, and releases it on the other. It moves solute only down its gradient and needs no metabolic energy, but there are a limited number of carriers, each with a finite cycling rate, so the rate approaches a maximum (Vmax, or Tmax in the kidney) as concentration rises. Carriers are also specific (D-glucose but not L-glucose) and open to competition by similar molecules. The GLUT family shows how carrier properties fit tissue roles: GLUT1 gives steady uptake into red cells and across the blood-brain barrier, GLUT2 has a high Km so that liver and pancreatic beta cell uptake tracks blood glucose, and insulin moves GLUT4 into the membrane of skeletal muscle and adipocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary active transport hydrolyzes ATP at the transporter itself to move a solute uphill: the Na+/K+-ATPase, the plasma membrane and sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPases, and the gastric H+/K+-ATPase. Secondary active transport couples the downhill entry of Na+ to the uphill movement of another solute. In cotransport (symport) both move the same way, as when SGLT1 carries 2 Na+ with 1 glucose or galactose into the enterocyte. In countertransport (antiport) they move in opposite directions, as with the Na+/H+ exchanger and the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (3 Na+ in for 1 Ca2+ out). The carrier hydrolyzes no ATP, but it fails whenever the Na+/K+-ATPase fails. Both active forms are saturable: glucose appears in the urine when plasma glucose exceeds about 180 to 200 mg/dL (10 to 11 mmol/L) because proximal tubule SGLT2 and SGLT1 are then working at capacity."},{"type":"p","text":"Vesicular transport moves fluid, macromolecules, and particles and needs ATP. Pinocytosis takes in extracellular fluid, phagocytosis engulfs particles such as bacteria, and receptor-mediated endocytosis concentrates ligands in clathrin-coated pits, as when LDL receptors take up LDL. Exocytosis releases vesicle contents, usually triggered by a rise in cytosolic Ca2+."},{"type":"table","title":"Membrane transport compared","cols":["Mechanism","Energy source","Direction","Saturable","Example"],"rows":[["Simple diffusion","None (the gradient itself)","Down gradient","No","O2 and CO2 across the lipid"],["Facilitated diffusion","None","Down gradient","Yes","GLUT1 in red cells; GLUT4 in muscle and fat"],["Primary active","ATP hydrolyzed by the transporter","Against gradient","Yes","Na+/K+-ATPase (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in)"],["Secondary active","Na+ gradient built by the Na+/K+-ATPase","Against gradient for the coupled solute","Yes","SGLT1 (2 Na+ with 1 glucose); Na+/Ca2+ exchanger"],["Endocytosis","ATP","Into the cell in vesicles","Yes when receptor-mediated","LDL receptor in clathrin-coated pits"]],"caption":"Carrier-mediated and receptor-mediated processes saturate because the number of binding sites is limited; simple diffusion does not."},{"type":"flow","title":"Glucose absorption across the enterocyte","steps":["Basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps Na+ out, keeping cell Na+ low","Apical SGLT1 carries 2 Na+ and 1 glucose into the cell","Glucose rises inside the cell above its plasma level","Basolateral GLUT2 releases glucose by facilitated diffusion","Glucose enters portal blood"],"caption":"Primary active, secondary active, and facilitated transport working in series across one epithelial cell."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C03","obj":"IN-3.3","title":"The Na+/K+-ATPase, cell volume, and hypoxic cell swelling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Na+/K+-ATPase is a P-type ATPase. Its alpha subunit carries the ion binding sites, the ATP site, and an extracellular site where cardiac glycosides bind; its beta subunit is needed for assembly and delivery to the membrane. Each cycle hydrolyzes one ATP and moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in, both against their gradients, so it exports one net positive charge and is electrogenic. It keeps intracellular Na+ near 10 to 15 mmol/L and K+ near 140 mmol/L, and it consumes a large share of a cell's resting ATP, especially in neurons."},{"type":"p","text":"The pump is also the cell's volume regulator. Cells contain impermeant anionic proteins and organic phosphates that draw in cations and water (the Gibbs-Donnan effect, C05). The membrane leaks Na+ slowly, and by expelling Na+ as fast as it enters, the pump makes Na+ behave as if it were an impermeant extracellular solute that balances the pull of the trapped anions. This is the pump-leak model of steady cell volume."},{"type":"p","text":"When the pump stops, the consequences unfold at different speeds. Its small electrogenic contribution disappears at once, so the membrane depolarizes (becomes less negative) by a few millivolts. Over minutes, Na+ leaks in and K+ leaks out down gradients that are no longer restored, Cl- enters as the interior becomes less negative, and water follows the net gain of solute, so the cell swells. As the K+ gradient runs down, E(K) becomes less negative and the membrane depolarizes further, first raising and then lowering excitability (C06). Partial inhibition by a cardiac glycoside such as digoxin shows a milder form in cardiac myocytes: higher intracellular Na+ weakens the gradient that drives Ca2+ out through the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, more Ca2+ is stored and released by the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and contractility rises. Digoxin competes with K+ at its binding site, which is why hypokalemia increases its binding."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicted effects of stopping the Na+/K+-ATPase","cols":["Variable","Change","Reason"],"rows":[["Intracellular Na+","Rises","Na+ leak continues without removal"],["Intracellular K+","Falls","K+ leaks out and is not pumped back"],["Cell volume","Increases","Na+ and Cl- gain draws water in"],["Membrane potential","Depolarizes","Loss of electrogenic current, then a smaller K+ gradient"],["Intracellular Ca2+","Rises","Weaker Na+ gradient slows Ca2+ extrusion by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger"]],"caption":"The same pattern follows a pump inhibitor, cold, or a lack of ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxic injury reaches the same chain by removing ATP instead of blocking the pump. Without oxygen, oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, the pump slows, and Na+ and water enter. Anaerobic glycolysis briefly supplies ATP but depletes glycogen and produces lactic acid, lowering intracellular pH and clumping nuclear chromatin, and ribosomes detach from the rough endoplasmic reticulum, so protein synthesis falls. Under the microscope this is cellular swelling, or hydropic change: enlarged, pale cells with small clear vacuoles of distended endoplasmic reticulum. It is the earliest change of reversible injury and resolves if oxygen returns. Sustained Ca2+ influx, severe mitochondrial damage, and loss of membrane integrity mark the move toward irreversible injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic cell swelling","steps":["Hypoxia halts oxidative phosphorylation; ATP falls","Na+/K+-ATPase slows","Na+ accumulates inside; K+ leaks out","Water follows the gained solute into the cell","Hydropic swelling, membrane blebs, swollen mitochondria, dilated ER","Reversible if oxygen and ATP are restored"],"caption":"The causal chain from ATP depletion to the first visible sign of reversible cell injury."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Red cells stored for transfusion at 4 °C (39 °F) have almost no Na+/K+-ATPase activity. Predict their intracellular K+, the K+ concentration of the surrounding fluid, and their volume.","explain":"With the pump stopped by cold, K+ leaks out and Na+ leaks in, so cell K+ falls, the K+ of the storage fluid rises, and Na+, Cl-, and water entry swell the cells.","check":"Compare your answer with the table of predicted effects of stopping the pump."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C04","obj":"IN-3.4","title":"Osmosis, osmolarity, and tonicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osmosis is net diffusion of water across a membrane that is more permeable to water than to solute, from the side with fewer solute particles toward the side with more. Water crosses the lipid bilayer slowly and crosses much faster through aquaporin channels. The pressure needed to stop osmosis is the osmotic pressure, which by the van't Hoff relation is proportional to the concentration of osmotically active particles. At body temperature each 1 mOsm/L difference corresponds to about 19.3 mmHg, so even small solute differences move large amounts of water."},{"type":"p","text":"Osmolarity is osmoles per liter of solution, and osmolality is osmoles per kilogram of water; in body fluids they are nearly equal, and laboratories measure osmolality by freezing point depression. Both count every dissolved particle, so 1 mmol of NaCl gives close to 2 mOsm because it dissociates. Normal plasma osmolality is about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg and can be estimated as 2 x Na+ (mmol/L) + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8, with glucose and BUN in mg/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Tonicity is a different question: what will this solution do to cell volume? Only effective osmoles count, meaning solutes that cannot cross the cell membrane (reflection coefficient near 1). Water moves until the effective osmolality inside and outside is equal, and cells hold about 290 mOsm/kg of effectively impermeant solute, mostly K+ with its anions. A solution with the same effective osmolality is isotonic, one with less is hypotonic and swells cells, and one with more is hypertonic and shrinks them. Because cells behave approximately as osmometers, their water volume changes in inverse proportion to the effective osmolality around them."},{"type":"p","text":"A permeant solute adds to osmolarity but not to tonicity. Urea crosses most cell membranes quickly, through the lipid and through urea transporters, so its reflection coefficient is near 0: it reaches the same concentration inside and outside and exerts no lasting pull. Na+ with its accompanying anions sits at the other extreme, with a coefficient near 1. An isosmotic urea solution therefore acts like pure water for a red cell: urea diffuses in, water follows, and the cell swells and lyses. In the body, a high BUN in renal failure raises measured osmolality without shrinking cells, and ethanol behaves the same way. By contrast, a rise in plasma Na+, or in glucose when insulin action is lacking, draws water out of cells, which is why hyperglycemia lowers plasma Na+ by dilution."},{"type":"table","title":"Cell volume in different solutions","cols":["Solution","Osmolarity","Effective osmolality vs cell","Water movement","Cell volume"],"rows":[["0.9% NaCl","About 308 mOsm/L calculated (behaves as about 286)","Equal","None","No change"],["0.45% NaCl","About 154 mOsm/L","Lower","Into cell","Swells"],["3% NaCl","About 1,026 mOsm/L","Higher","Out of cell","Shrinks"],["300 mOsm/L urea","Isosmotic","Lower (urea enters the cell)","Into cell","Swells and lyses"],["150 mOsm/L NaCl plus 150 mOsm/L urea","Isosmotic","Lower (only the NaCl counts)","Into cell","Swells, as in 0.45% NaCl"]],"caption":"Osmolarity counts all particles; tonicity, and therefore cell volume, depends only on the solutes that stay outside."},{"type":"example","title":"Isosmotic is not the same as isotonic","text":"A red cell with an effective osmolality of 290 mOsm/kg is placed in a solution of 150 mOsm/L NaCl plus 150 mOsm/L urea. Step 1: total osmolarity is 300 mOsm/L, so the solution is isosmotic. Step 2: urea crosses the membrane and equalizes, so it contributes nothing to tonicity; the effective osmolality outside is 150. Step 3: water enters until the inside also reaches about 150, which requires the cell water to nearly double (290/150 is about 1.9). Step 4: a red cell has a fixed surface area and can swell only until it becomes a sphere, so it lyses before reaching that volume. The solution is isosmotic but hypotonic."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C05","obj":"IN-3.5","title":"Equilibrium potentials and the resting membrane potential","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"If a membrane is permeable to one ion only, that ion diffuses down its concentration gradient and carries charge with it, building a voltage that opposes further movement. The equilibrium (Nernst) potential is the voltage at which the electrical force exactly balances the chemical force, so net flux stops. Only a tiny number of ions must cross to create this voltage, so bulk concentrations are essentially unchanged."},{"type":"p","text":"At 37 °C (98.6 °F) the Nernst equation simplifies to E = (61/z) x log10([ion]out/[ion]in) in millivolts, where z is the valence and E is the inside relative to the outside. A cation concentrated outside has a positive E, one concentrated inside a negative E, and for an anion the sign flips because z is negative. A tenfold gradient gives about 61 mV for a monovalent ion and about 30 mV for a divalent ion."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical concentrations and equilibrium potentials","cols":["Ion","Outside (mmol/L)","Inside (mmol/L)","Equilibrium potential"],"rows":[["K+","4","140","About -94 mV"],["Na+","142","14","About +61 mV"],["Cl-","About 105","About 5 to 10 (varies by cell)","About -60 to -80 mV"],["Ca2+ (ionized)","About 1.2","About 0.0001","About +120 to +130 mV"]],"caption":"Values follow Guyton for a large nerve fiber; intracellular Cl- and therefore E(Cl) vary between cell types."},{"type":"p","text":"Real membranes pass several ions, so the potential is a weighted compromise given by the Goldman equation: Vm = 61 x log10[(PK[K]out + PNa[Na]out + PCl[Cl]in) / (PK[K]in + PNa[Na]in + PCl[Cl]out)], where P is permeability. The Cl- terms are inverted because Cl- is negative. The potential lies closest to the equilibrium potential of the most permeable ion and changes whenever permeability changes, not only when concentrations change. That is the basis of the action potential, in which a surge of Na+ permeability pulls Vm toward E(Na)."},{"type":"p","text":"At rest, K+ leak channels (two-pore domain and inward rectifier) are open, and K+ permeability is about 100 times Na+ permeability in Guyton's large nerve fiber. The potential therefore sits near E(K), pulled slightly positive by the Na+ leak. This is a steady state, not an equilibrium: the leaks continue, and only the pump keeps the gradients from running down. Guyton builds the value in steps: K+ diffusion alone gives about -94 mV, the Na+ leak brings it to about -86 mV, and the electrogenic pump adds about -4 mV, totaling about -90 mV. Typical resting values are about -70 mV in many neurons and about -90 mV in skeletal and ventricular muscle. In many cells Cl- distributes passively, so E(Cl) lies near Vm and Cl- follows the potential rather than setting it."},{"type":"p","text":"The Gibbs-Donnan effect explains a further constraint. Proteins and organic phosphates are trapped inside and mostly negatively charged at body pH. To keep electroneutrality they hold extra diffusible cations inside and push diffusible anions out, until the products of the diffusible ion concentrations are equal on both sides ([K+]in x [Cl-]in = [K+]out x [Cl-]out). The result is a small inside-negative Donnan potential and more osmotically active particles inside than outside, so water would keep entering until the cell burst. The Na+/K+-ATPase prevents this by keeping Na+ effectively outside (C03)."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating E(K) and seeing why extracellular K+ matters","text":"With K+ of 4 mmol/L outside and 140 inside, the ratio is 4/140 = 0.029, whose log10 is about -1.54; multiplied by 61 this gives E(K) of about -94 mV. Now raise extracellular K+ to 7 mmol/L. The ratio becomes 7/140 = 0.05, log10 about -1.30, and E(K) about -79 mV. A rise of only 3 mmol/L outside moves E(K) about 15 mV toward zero, and because the resting potential tracks E(K), the cell depolarizes by a similar amount. A 3 mmol/L fall inside, from 140 to 137, would change E(K) by less than 1 mV."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C06","obj":"IN-3.6","title":"Potassium, resting potential, and excitability","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because resting K+ permeability is high, the resting potential tracks E(K), and E(K) is far more sensitive to extracellular than intracellular K+. Intracellular K+ is about 140 mmol/L, while plasma K+ is normally only about 3.5 to 5.0 mmol/L, so a change of a few mmol/L outside alters the ratio greatly (C05 example). Only about 2% of total body K+ is extracellular, so shifts between cells and plasma can change plasma K+ quickly."},{"type":"p","text":"In hyperkalemia the K+ gradient shrinks, E(K) becomes less negative, and the resting membrane depolarizes. A small depolarization brings the membrane closer to threshold, which is the voltage at which inward Na+ current first exceeds outward K+ current and depolarization becomes self-sustaining, so excitability may rise briefly. Sustained depolarization does the opposite because of how voltage-gated Na+ channels behave. Each has an activation gate that opens quickly on depolarization and an inactivation gate that closes more slowly and stays closed while the membrane remains depolarized; it reopens only after the membrane repolarizes to a sufficiently negative voltage. In a chronically depolarized cell a growing fraction of Na+ channels is therefore inactivated. Fewer available channels give a slower, smaller upstroke, slower conduction, and finally failure to fire, so skeletal muscle weakens and can become paralyzed even though the voltage sits closer to threshold."},{"type":"p","text":"In the heart, the slower phase 0 upstroke widens the QRS, and slowed atrial conduction flattens the P wave. High extracellular K+ also increases the conductance of several K+ channels, so repolarization speeds up and T waves become tall and peaked. At severe levels the QRS merges into the T wave in a sine wave pattern before ventricular fibrillation or asystole."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypokalemia works the other way: the gradient is larger, E(K) is more negative, and the membrane hyperpolarizes, so a larger stimulus is needed to reach threshold. Skeletal muscle weakness and cramps and smooth muscle hypomotility with ileus follow. In the heart, low extracellular K+ reduces K+ channel conductance, which delays repolarization: T waves flatten, U waves become prominent, and early and delayed afterdepolarizations make ectopic beats more likely. When K+ conductance falls far enough, cardiac cells can paradoxically depolarize, an exception to the simple Goldman prediction. Extracellular Ca2+ acts by a separate mechanism: it binds negative charges near Na+ channels and shifts their opening to more depolarized voltages, so hypocalcemia lowers the threshold and causes tetany without changing the resting potential much."},{"type":"table","title":"Hyperkalemia and hypokalemia compared","cols":["Feature","Hyperkalemia (above about 5.0 mmol/L)","Hypokalemia (below about 3.5 mmol/L)"],"rows":[["E(K)","Less negative","More negative"],["Resting potential","Depolarized","Hyperpolarized (paradoxical depolarization if severe in heart)"],["Na+ channel availability","Reduced by inactivation","Normal or increased"],["Nerve and skeletal muscle","Brief hyperexcitability, then weakness and paralysis","Weakness, cramps; ileus in smooth muscle"],["Cardiac repolarization","Faster (higher K+ conductance)","Slower (lower K+ conductance)"],["ECG","Peaked T, flat P, wide QRS, sine wave","Flat T, prominent U waves, ectopic beats"]],"caption":"Both extremes reduce excitability of nerve and skeletal muscle, by different mechanisms."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sustained hyperkalemia to weakness","steps":["Extracellular K+ rises","E(K) becomes less negative","Resting membrane depolarizes","Na+ channel inactivation gates close and stay closed","Fewer Na+ channels available to open","Slower upstroke, slowed conduction, failure to fire","Muscle weakness, wide QRS, arrhythmia"],"caption":"Why a membrane closer to threshold becomes less excitable, not more."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Plasma K+ rises from 4 to 8 mmol/L over a day. Predict the resting potential of a skeletal muscle fiber, the number of Na+ channels available to open, and muscle strength.","explain":"E(K) and the resting potential become less negative; sustained depolarization keeps inactivation gates closed, so fewer Na+ channels are available, action potentials fail, and strength falls.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on inactivation gates and the hyperkalemia flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C07","obj":"IN-3.7","title":"CFTR in the sweat duct and the airway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) is encoded on chromosome 7 and belongs to the ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter family, but it works as an anion channel. It opens when cAMP-dependent protein kinase A phosphorylates its regulatory domain and ATP binds its nucleotide-binding domains. It conducts Cl- and HCO3- and also regulates other transporters, notably the epithelial Na+ channel (ENaC). As a channel it does not choose a direction: Cl- moves down its electrochemical gradient, which points inward in an absorbing epithelium and outward in a secreting one."},{"type":"p","text":"The eccrine sweat gland has two parts. The secretory coil makes a primary fluid nearly isotonic with plasma; on cholinergic stimulation its Cl- secretion relies largely on Ca2+-activated Cl- channels, so people with cystic fibrosis (CF) produce a fairly normal volume of sweat. In the duct, cells reabsorb Cl- through apical CFTR and Na+ through ENaC, and because the duct is relatively impermeable to water, salt is removed without water and the sweat reaching the skin is hypotonic. In CF, Cl- cannot be reabsorbed. Na+ reabsorption also falls, because the retained Cl- leaves the lumen more negative and because ENaC activity in the duct depends on working CFTR, so the sweat stays salty. A sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher supports CF, 30 to 59 mmol/L is intermediate, and below 30 mmol/L makes CF unlikely. Heavy sweating can deplete salt, producing hyponatremic, hypochloremic dehydration with metabolic alkalosis."},{"type":"p","text":"In the airway the same channel secretes. Basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporters, powered by the Na+ gradient, load the cell with Cl-, and apical CFTR lets it flow into the lumen; Na+ follows between the cells and water follows the salt. This keeps the periciliary liquid deep enough for cilia to beat. In CF, Cl- and HCO3- secretion fail and ENaC-mediated Na+ absorption increases, so salt and water are drawn out of the airway surface liquid. Mucus becomes dehydrated, acidic, and poorly expanded, cilia are flattened, and clearance fails. Retained mucus is colonized, commonly first by Staphylococcus aureus and Haemophilus influenzae and later by mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and neutrophil-driven inflammation leads to bronchiectasis. The abnormally negative nasal potential difference in CF reflects this excess Na+ absorption."},{"type":"table","title":"One channel, opposite jobs","cols":["Feature","Sweat duct","Airway epithelium"],"rows":[["Normal CFTR role","Cl- reabsorption from lumen","Cl- and HCO3- secretion into lumen"],["ENaC in CF","Decreased","Increased"],["Water movement","Little; duct is water-tight","Follows salt across the epithelium"],["Result in CF","Salty sweat; salt depletion with heavy sweating","Dehydrated mucus, infection, bronchiectasis"],["Measured finding","Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or higher","More negative nasal potential difference"]],"caption":"Loss of CFTR raises salt in sweat but removes water from airway mucus."},{"type":"p","text":"The same failure of secretion plugs other ducts: the pancreas (exocrine insufficiency with fat malabsorption and deficient vitamins A, D, E, and K), the newborn ileum (meconium ileus), bile ductules (focal biliary cirrhosis), and the vas deferens, which is absent on both sides in nearly all affected males. CF is autosomal recessive. The most common mutation, F508del, misfolds the protein, which is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum and degraded, so little CFTR reaches the membrane."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cystic fibrosis airway disease","steps":["CFTR mutation such as F508del; protein degraded","Airway cells cannot secrete Cl- and HCO3-","ENaC Na+ absorption increases","Airway surface liquid depleted; mucus dehydrates","Mucociliary clearance fails","Chronic infection and neutrophil inflammation cause bronchiectasis"],"caption":"From a misfolded channel to structural airway damage."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with CF receives a cholinergic stimulus to the skin. Predict the volume of sweat and its chloride concentration compared with a healthy child.","explain":"Volume is near normal because the coil secretes through Ca2+-activated Cl- channels, but chloride is high because the duct cannot reabsorb Cl- through CFTR.","check":"Reread the sweat gland paragraph and the sweat duct column of the table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Membrane transport links every Part I discipline through one principle: the ion gradients that the Na+/K+-ATPase builds are the energy supply for volume control, nutrient uptake, and electrical signaling. Biochemistry explains why fatty acid saturation and cholesterol change fluidity and why hypoxia removes the ATP that powers the pump. Physiology turns gradients into numbers with the Nernst and Goldman equations and predicts cell volume from effective osmoles. Pathology reads the same chain backward, from hydropic swelling to pump failure, and from a wide QRS to K+-driven Na+ channel inactivation. Genetics and microbiology meet in cystic fibrosis, where a misfolded channel changes salt movement in opposite directions in two epithelia and leaves the airway open to chronic infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits matter. The Goldman equation assumes steady permeabilities, so it cannot predict paradoxical depolarization when K+ conductance itself falls in severe hypokalemia. Cells are not perfect osmometers, because part of their volume is solid and many regulate volume actively over minutes to hours. Reference values for intracellular ions, resting potentials, and sweat chloride vary between sources and cell types, so learn the direction of each change and the reasoning rather than a single number."}],"summary":["Cis double bonds and short fatty acid tails increase membrane fluidity; cholesterol buffers fluidity, lowering it when warm and preventing gel formation when cold.","Simple diffusion is not saturable; facilitated diffusion and primary and secondary active transport use a limited number of carriers and saturate at a maximum rate.","SGLT1 and SGLT2 use no ATP themselves but depend on the Na+ gradient built by the Na+/K+-ATPase; GLUT carriers move glucose only down its gradient.","The Na+/K+-ATPase moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP; stopping it raises cell Na+, swells the cell, and depolarizes the membrane.","ATP loss in hypoxia stops the pump, and Na+ and water entry produces hydropic swelling, the earliest sign of reversible injury.","Osmolarity counts all particles; tonicity counts only effective osmoles, so urea raises osmolarity but not tonicity, and isosmotic urea lyses red cells.","E = (61/z) x log(out/in); E(K) is about -94 mV and E(Na) about +61 mV, and the resting potential lies near E(K) because resting K+ permeability is highest.","Trapped intracellular anions (Gibbs-Donnan effect) would swell the cell; the pump prevents this by keeping Na+ effectively outside.","Hyperkalemia depolarizes and, if sustained, inactivates Na+ channels, lowering excitability; hypokalemia hyperpolarizes and slows cardiac repolarization.","CFTR reabsorbs Cl- in the sweat duct and secretes Cl- in the airway, so CF gives sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher and dehydrated airway mucus."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Substances Through Cell Membranes"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of solutes and water; electrophysiology of the cell membrane"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Phospholipid and cholesterol structure and membrane lipids"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell injury and cell death; cystic fibrosis in genetic and pediatric diseases"}]},"IN-4":{"unit":"IN-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01","title":"Thermoregulation","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the hypothalamus holds core temperature near its set point by balancing heat loss through the skin against heat conservation and production, and what happens when that balance fails. It covers the hypothalamic controller, the physics of heat loss, cold defenses including brown fat, fever, heat illness, malignant hyperthermia, hypothermia, and cold injury. Board questions turn on a few distinctions: whether the set point has moved (fever) or the controller is overwhelmed (hyperthermia), whether thermoregulation still works (heat exhaustion) or has failed (heat stroke), and which route of heat loss remains when the environment is hotter than the skin.","prerequisites":["Sympathetic adrenergic and cholinergic receptors and their second messengers (NE-9)","Chemiosmotic coupling of electron transport to ATP synthase","Skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling, DHP receptor, RYR1, and SERCA (MS-7)","Innate immune recognition, cytokines, and the cyclooxygenase pathway (IM-6, IM-13)","Cardiac action potential and the normal ECG (CV-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hypothalamic and limbic function (NE-12)","how":"The preoptic and posterior hypothalamic nuclei that set and defend core temperature sit beside the centers for thirst, feeding, and pituitary control, so one lesion often disturbs several of these."},{"to":"Skin layers and adnexa (IN-1)","how":"Eccrine sweat glands, arrector pili muscles, dermal arteriovenous anastomoses, and subcutaneous fat are the effectors and insulation this chapter puts to work."},{"to":"Muscle contraction (MS-7)","how":"Shivering, malignant hyperthermia, and exertional rhabdomyolysis are all explained by how skeletal muscle handles Ca2+ and ATP."},{"to":"Cell and cytokine signaling (IM-6)","how":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha released in infection and injury reset the hypothalamic set point through PGE2."},{"to":"Conduction system and ECG (CV-7)","how":"Cooling slows pacemaker and conduction currents, producing bradycardia, interval prolongation, Osborn waves, and fibrillation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Core temperature","def":"Temperature of the deep tissues (brain, thoracic and abdominal organs), held near about 37 °C (98.6 °F) while skin temperature varies."},{"term":"Set point","def":"The core temperature the hypothalamus defends; signals above it trigger heat loss and signals below it trigger heat conservation and production."},{"term":"Preoptic area","def":"Anterior hypothalamic region containing warm-sensitive neurons; it acts as the heat loss center."},{"term":"Evaporative heat loss","def":"Heat removed as water changes to vapor on skin and airways, about 0.58 kcal per gram, requiring a water vapor pressure gradient rather than a temperature gradient."},{"term":"Nonshivering thermogenesis","def":"Heat production without muscle contraction, mainly by sympathetic activation of UCP1 in brown fat."},{"term":"UCP1 (thermogenin)","def":"Inner mitochondrial membrane protein of brown adipocytes that lets protons re-enter the matrix without passing through ATP synthase, releasing the gradient as heat."},{"term":"Endogenous pyrogen","def":"A host cytokine, chiefly IL-1, IL-6, or TNF-alpha, that raises the hypothalamic set point."},{"term":"Fever","def":"A regulated rise in core temperature caused by an increase in the set point."},{"term":"Hyperthermia","def":"A rise in core temperature with an unchanged set point, because heat gain or production exceeds heat loss."},{"term":"Heat stroke","def":"Core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with central nervous system dysfunction."},{"term":"Malignant hyperthermia","def":"Inherited skeletal muscle hypermetabolic crisis, usually from an RYR1 mutation, triggered by volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine."},{"term":"Osborn (J) wave","def":"A positive deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment that appears and grows as core temperature falls."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C01","obj":"IN-4.1","title":"The hypothalamic thermostat","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Core temperature stays within about 0.6 °C (1 °F) of its set point across a wide range of environments, while skin temperature rises and falls with the surroundings. Oral core temperature averages about 37 °C (98.6 °F), with a daily rhythm that is lowest in the early morning and highest in the late afternoon; rectal readings run a few tenths of a degree higher. The controller is the hypothalamus, which works as a negative feedback thermostat: it compares sensed temperature with the set point and drives heat loss or heat gain to cancel the difference."},{"type":"p","text":"Two sets of sensors feed it. Core sensing occurs mainly in the preoptic and anterior hypothalamus, where warm-sensitive neurons increase their firing as the temperature of the blood perfusing them rises; they outnumber cold-sensitive neurons there by about three to one. Deep receptors in the spinal cord, abdominal viscera, and great veins add core information. Skin thermoreceptors, mostly cold receptors (several times more numerous than warm receptors), are free nerve endings with temperature-gated TRP channels such as TRPM8 for cooling. Their signals reach the posterior hypothalamus, which integrates them with the preoptic signals. Skin input lets the body respond before the core changes, so stepping into cold air triggers vasoconstriction and shivering while core temperature is still normal."},{"type":"p","text":"The effector side is divided. The preoptic and anterior hypothalamus is the heat loss center: when sensed temperature exceeds the set point, it withdraws sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone to the skin, activates active cutaneous vasodilation, and drives sweating through sympathetic cholinergic fibers acting on M3 receptors of eccrine glands. It also inhibits the heat production centers. The posterior hypothalamus is the heat conservation and production center: when temperature falls below the set point, it drives sympathetic cutaneous vasoconstriction, piloerection, shivering from the primary motor center for shivering in its dorsomedial portion, and sympathetic and thyroid driven thermogenesis. Behavioral responses, such as seeking shelter or adding clothing, are the most powerful of all and depend on conscious perception of comfort."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Negative feedback against a rise in core temperature","steps":["Core temperature rises above the set point","Preoptic warm-sensitive neurons fire faster","Skin vasodilation and sweating increase","Heat loss exceeds heat production","Core temperature falls toward the set point","Warm-sensitive firing returns to baseline"],"caption":"The same loop runs in reverse in the cold, with reduced warm-sensitive firing releasing posterior hypothalamic vasoconstriction and shivering."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypothalamic regions in temperature control","cols":["Region","Main role","Responses driven","Effect of a lesion"],"rows":[["Preoptic and anterior hypothalamus","Core sensing; heat loss center","Cutaneous vasodilation, sweating, inhibition of heat production","Hyperthermia in a warm environment, because heat cannot be shed"],["Posterior hypothalamus","Integrates skin and core input; heat conservation and production center","Cutaneous vasoconstriction, piloerection, shivering, thermogenesis","Poikilothermia: core temperature drifts toward ambient, most dangerously in the cold"]],"caption":"Each region controls one half of the response, so the lesion site predicts which extreme the patient cannot tolerate."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the halves are anatomically separate, lesions are predictable. Anterior damage from stroke, trauma, tumor, or surgery removes heat loss and produces hyperthermia. A posterior lesion destroys the heat conservation centers and also interrupts descending pathways from the anterior region, so the patient becomes poikilothermic. A high cervical cord transection cuts hypothalamic control of sympathetic vasomotor tone and sweating below the lesion, impairing defense in both heat and cold."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient recovering from surgery for a craniopharyngioma that damaged the posterior hypothalamus is placed in a cool room. What happens to core temperature, and why?","explain":"Core temperature falls toward room temperature, because the posterior centers that trigger vasoconstriction and shivering are gone and descending heat loss pathways are also interrupted, leaving poikilothermia.","check":"Compare with the table of hypothalamic regions and the paragraph on lesions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C02","obj":"IN-4.2","title":"Routes of heat loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood carries core heat to the skin, so skin blood flow sets how fast heat reaches the surface. Skin flow ranges from nearly zero to about 30% of cardiac output, much of it through arteriovenous anastomoses (direct shunts from arterioles to venules) in the hands, feet, ears, and nose. Once heat reaches the skin, four physical routes remove it. At rest in a comfortable room, about 60% of heat loss is by radiation, 15% by conduction to air with convection, 3% by conduction to objects, and 22% by evaporation (Guyton and Hall)."},{"type":"p","text":"Radiation is the emission of infrared energy from the skin to cooler surfaces that are not in contact. It works in both directions, so the body also absorbs radiant heat from the sun and hot surfaces. Conduction is direct transfer of heat between surfaces in contact, such as skin and a cold bench or the thin layer of air touching the skin; once that air layer warms, conduction stops unless the layer is replaced. Convection is that replacement: moving air or water carries the warmed layer away, so wind and immersion multiply heat loss. Water conducts heat far better than air, so immersion cools much faster than air at the same temperature."},{"type":"p","text":"Evaporation works differently. Converting water to vapor absorbs its latent heat, about 0.58 kcal per gram, so it cools the skin even when the surroundings are warmer than the body. Insensible loss through skin and airways (about 600 to 700 mL per day) runs continuously, and sweating adds a regulated route."},{"type":"table","title":"The four routes of heat loss","cols":["Route","Physical basis","Driving gradient","When it fails or reverses"],"rows":[["Radiation","Infrared emission to surfaces not in contact","Skin warmer than surrounding surfaces","Reverses in sun or near hot surfaces"],["Conduction","Direct transfer between touching surfaces","Skin warmer than the object or air in contact","Reverses in hot air or water"],["Convection","Moving air or water replaces the warmed layer","Skin warmer than the moving fluid","Adds heat when air or water is hotter than skin"],["Evaporation","Latent heat of vaporization, about 0.58 kcal per gram","Water vapor pressure at wet skin higher than in air","Fails in high humidity or when sweating is impaired"]],"caption":"Only evaporation is independent of the temperature gradient, so it is the only route left in a hot environment."},{"type":"p","text":"This leads to the key prediction. Radiation, conduction, and convection move heat from warmer to cooler. When ambient temperature, or the temperature of radiating surroundings, exceeds skin temperature (about 33 °C (91.4 °F) in comfortable conditions and up to about 35 °C (95 °F) in the heat), those three routes add heat to the body, and evaporation of sweat becomes the only way to lose heat. High humidity shrinks the vapor pressure gradient, so sweat accumulates and drips off without evaporating; it removes no heat, yet the water and salt are still lost from plasma. Anything that impairs sweating, such as anticholinergic drugs, extensive scarring or burns, ectodermal dysplasia, or dehydration, removes the last route. Acclimatization over about one to six weeks roughly doubles maximal sweat rate, and aldosterone lowers sweat NaCl so less salt is lost per liter."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a humid day overheats a runner","text":"A 70 kg runner produces about 800 kcal per hour in air hotter than the skin, so evaporation must remove all of it. Evaporating 1.4 L of sweat removes about 800 kcal (1,400 g times 0.58 kcal), so in dry air 1.4 L per hour keeps balance. In very humid air, suppose only half evaporates: about 400 kcal per hour is stored. Tissue needs about 0.83 kcal per kg per °C, or about 58 kcal per °C for this runner, so core temperature climbs about 7 °C per hour and reaches heat stroke range within about 30 minutes, while the full 1.4 L is still lost from plasma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C03","obj":"IN-4.3","title":"Heat conservation and heat production","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the cold, the posterior hypothalamus first reduces heat loss and then increases heat production. Sympathetic norepinephrine acting on alpha-adrenergic receptors constricts cutaneous arterioles and closes arteriovenous anastomoses. Warm blood stays in the core, and skin and subcutaneous fat become an insulating shell. In the limbs, deep arteries run beside their accompanying veins, so cooled venous blood is rewarmed by arterial blood (countercurrent exchange) and heat returns to the core. Piloerection (arrector pili contraction through alpha-1 receptors) traps air in furred mammals but adds little in humans."},{"type":"p","text":"When conservation is not enough, the primary motor center for shivering in the dorsomedial posterior hypothalamus, normally inhibited by the anterior heat center, sends descending signals to spinal motor neurons. Tone rises, then agonist and antagonist muscles contract rhythmically. Because almost no external work is done, nearly all the ATP energy used appears as heat, and maximal shivering can raise heat production about four to five fold. Shivering depends on glycogen, glucose, and oxygen, and it fails when fuel runs out or when the core cools enough to depress the controller (C07)."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonshivering (chemical) thermogenesis takes place mainly in brown adipose tissue. Sympathetic norepinephrine acts on beta-3 receptors of brown adipocytes, and Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A activate hormone-sensitive lipase. The released fatty acids serve both as fuel for beta oxidation and as activators of UCP1 (thermogenin) in the inner mitochondrial membrane. UCP1 gives protons a path back into the matrix that bypasses ATP synthase, so the energy of the proton gradient is released as heat. With the gradient continuously dissipated, electron transport is no longer held back by ADP availability and runs at a high rate. Brown adipocytes have many small lipid droplets, abundant cytochrome-rich mitochondria (the brown color), dense capillaries, and direct sympathetic innervation. Sympathetic stimulation also induces type 2 deiodinase in brown fat, raising local T3, which increases UCP1 expression."},{"type":"flow","title":"Nonshivering thermogenesis in brown fat","steps":["Cold sensed; sympathetic outflow increases","Norepinephrine binds beta-3 receptors on brown adipocytes","Gs, cAMP, and PKA activate hormone-sensitive lipase","Free fatty acids are released from lipid droplets","Fatty acids activate UCP1 and fuel beta oxidation","Protons re-enter the matrix, bypassing ATP synthase","Proton gradient energy is released as heat"],"caption":"Uncoupling converts oxidative energy to heat instead of ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormone acts more slowly. Prolonged cold increases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) and therefore thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and thyroid hormone output, and triiodothyronine (T3) raises basal metabolic rate by increasing Na+/K+ ATPase activity, mitochondrial oxidative capacity, and catecholamine sensitivity. The effect builds over days to weeks, supporting acclimatization, and explains cold intolerance in hypothyroidism and heat intolerance in hyperthyroidism."},{"type":"p","text":"Neonates depend on brown fat. They have a high surface area to mass ratio, little subcutaneous fat for insulation, and a limited ability to shiver, so they lose heat quickly and lack the adult's main production route. Brown fat, which accumulates in late gestation between the scapulae, in the neck and axillae, and around the kidneys, adrenals, and great vessels, can roughly double an infant's heat production, whereas chemical thermogenesis raises adult heat production only about 10 to 15%. Preterm infants have less brown fat and glycogen and cool fastest, and cold stress raises oxygen and glucose use, risking hypoxemia, hypoglycemia, and metabolic acidosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Responses to cold","cols":["Response","Effector and receptor","Speed and size"],"rows":[["Cutaneous vasoconstriction","Sympathetic NE on alpha receptors of skin arterioles and AV anastomoses","Seconds; first line"],["Piloerection","Sympathetic NE on alpha-1 receptors of arrector pili","Immediate; minor in humans"],["Shivering","Posterior hypothalamic motor center to somatic motor neurons","Minutes; up to about 4 to 5 fold heat production"],["Nonshivering thermogenesis","Sympathetic NE on beta-3 receptors of brown fat; UCP1 uncoupling","Minutes; major in neonates, small in adults"],["Thyroid hormone","TRH, TSH, and T3 raising basal metabolic rate","Days to weeks; acclimatization"]],"caption":"Heat conservation acts first; heat production adds as cold persists."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C04","obj":"IN-4.4","title":"Fever and hyperthermia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fever is a regulated rise in core temperature caused by an upward shift of the hypothalamic set point. It begins with exogenous pyrogens, microbial products such as lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of gram-negative bacteria, lipoteichoic acid, and other pathogen-associated molecular patterns. These bind pattern recognition receptors, for example LPS on TLR4 with CD14, on macrophages and other innate cells, which then release the endogenous pyrogens IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. Tissue injury, malignancy, and autoimmune disease release the same cytokines."},{"type":"p","text":"These cytokines do not freely cross the blood-brain barrier. They act on the endothelium of brain vessels and on the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT), a circumventricular organ beside the preoptic area whose capillaries are fenestrated. There they induce cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), and the prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) produced acts on EP3 receptors of preoptic neurons, reducing warm-sensitive neuron firing. The controller now reads a normal blood temperature as too cold. Cytokines do not heat the body directly; they change the reference value, and the body's own heat conservation and production then raise core temperature."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of fever","steps":["Microbial products bind Toll-like receptors on macrophages","Macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha","Cytokines act at the OVLT and brain endothelium","COX-2 induction increases PGE2 synthesis","PGE2 on preoptic EP3 receptors raises the set point","Vasoconstriction and shivering raise core temperature to the new set point"],"caption":"Fever is produced by the normal cold defenses responding to a higher target."},{"type":"p","text":"The phases follow from the moving set point. If the set point rises to 39 °C (102.2 °F), a core temperature of 37 °C (98.6 °F) is now below target, so the posterior hypothalamus drives skin vasoconstriction (pale skin), shivering (chills or rigors), and the feeling of cold, until core temperature reaches the new value. During the plateau, heat loss and production balance at the higher level. When the cytokine stimulus ends, or when cyclooxygenase inhibition by nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or acetaminophen lowers PGE2 synthesis, the set point falls. Core temperature is now above target, so the anterior hypothalamus drives sweating and cutaneous vasodilation (the flush or crisis). Fever itself is usually self-limited and rarely exceeds about 41 °C (105.8 °F), and each 1 °C of fever raises metabolic rate and oxygen consumption by roughly 10 to 13%, with matching rises in heart rate and ventilation."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperthermia is an elevated core temperature with an unchanged set point. Heat gain or production exceeds heat loss, so the controller is overwhelmed or bypassed while still trying to cool: the skin is usually flushed and sweating is maximal unless sweating itself is blocked. Causes fall into three groups: excess environmental heat (heat stroke), excess production (malignant hyperthermia, thyroid storm, sympathomimetic drugs such as cocaine and amphetamines, prolonged seizures, serotonin syndrome, neuroleptic malignant syndrome), and impaired loss (anticholinergic drugs, anterior hypothalamic lesions). Because PGE2 did not raise the set point, blocking PGE2 synthesis does not lower the temperature."},{"type":"table","title":"Fever compared with hyperthermia","cols":["Feature","Fever","Hyperthermia"],"rows":[["Set point","Raised by PGE2","Unchanged"],["Cause","Endogenous pyrogens (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha)","Heat gain or production exceeds loss, or loss is impaired"],["Hypothalamic response while temperature rises","Conserves and produces heat: vasoconstriction, chills, rigors","Tries to lose heat: vasodilation, sweating (if possible)"],["Usual ceiling","Rarely above about 41 °C (105.8 °F)","No regulated ceiling; can exceed 42 °C (107.6 °F)"],["Effect of COX inhibition","Lowers temperature","No effect"]],"caption":"The question to ask is whether the hypothalamus is trying to warm or to cool the body."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient's temperature is 39.5 °C (103.1 °F) and still rising. The skin is cold and pale, and the patient is shaking under blankets. Is this fever or hyperthermia?","explain":"Fever. The hypothalamus is actively conserving and producing heat, which means the set point is above the current core temperature. In hyperthermia the controller would be driving vasodilation and sweating.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on fever phases and the fever compared with hyperthermia table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C05","obj":"IN-4.5","title":"Heat exhaustion and heat stroke","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heat exhaustion is mainly a circulatory failure, with thermoregulation still working. Sweating depletes water and NaCl, and cutaneous vasodilation diverts a large share of cardiac output to the skin, so venous return, stroke volume, and blood pressure fall. Core temperature is raised but usually below 40 °C (104 °F), mental status is normal, and the person sweats heavily with weakness, headache, nausea, tachycardia, and orthostatic hypotension. The controller is intact; the circulation cannot supply both skin and organs."},{"type":"p","text":"Heat stroke is a core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with central nervous system dysfunction, such as confusion, delirium, ataxia, seizures, or coma. The CNS finding defines the diagnosis because the brain is the most heat sensitive organ. Heat at these levels denatures proteins, destabilizes membranes, and uncouples oxidative phosphorylation. A systemic inflammatory response amplifies the damage: blood shunted to the skin leaves the splanchnic bed ischemic, the gut barrier leaks endotoxin, and cytokines together with heat-injured endothelium activate coagulation."},{"type":"table","title":"Heat exhaustion, classic heat stroke, and exertional heat stroke","cols":["Feature","Heat exhaustion","Classic heat stroke","Exertional heat stroke"],"rows":[["Core temperature","Usually 37 to 40 °C (98.6 to 104 °F)","Above 40 °C (104 °F)","Above 40 °C (104 °F)"],["Mental status","Normal","Altered","Altered"],["Who","Anyone in heat with volume loss","Older adults, infants, chronically ill","Young healthy athletes, soldiers, laborers"],["Onset","Hours","Days, during heat waves","Hours of exertion"],["Main mechanism","Volume depletion and skin vasodilation","Impaired heat loss and reserve","Heat production exceeds loss"],["Skin","Moist, heavy sweating","Often hot and dry","Sweating often persists"],["Rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury (AKI), disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Absent","Possible, less common","Common and often severe"]],"caption":"CNS dysfunction, not a single temperature reading, separates heat stroke from heat exhaustion."},{"type":"p","text":"Classic heat stroke overwhelms people with reduced reserve: impaired thirst and mobility, limited ability to raise cardiac output, and drugs that block sweating (anticholinergics, many antipsychotics) or deplete volume (diuretics). Exertional heat stroke occurs in healthy people because muscle produces heat faster than it can be shed, especially in humid heat (C02); sweating often persists, so moist skin does not exclude it."},{"type":"p","text":"The complications follow from cell injury. Rhabdomyolysis is breakdown of skeletal muscle membranes from heat and ATP depletion, releasing creatine kinase, myoglobin, K+, phosphate, and urate. Filtered myoglobin forms casts with uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) in acidic tubular fluid and its heme iron injures proximal tubules, while volume depletion lowers glomerular filtration, producing acute kidney injury. Urine is dark and dipstick positive for blood with few red cells, because the test detects heme. DIC follows endothelial injury and tissue factor exposure: consumption of platelets and clotting factors gives low platelets, prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), low fibrinogen, and high D-dimer, with both bleeding and microthrombi. Hyperkalemia follows muscle lysis and AKI, and hepatocellular injury raises aminotransferases."},{"type":"flow","title":"From heat stroke to multiorgan injury","steps":["Heat gain exceeds heat loss","Core temperature rises above 40 °C (104 °F)","Proteins denature; neurons fail, causing CNS dysfunction","Splanchnic ischemia lets gut endotoxin enter blood","Cytokines and endothelial injury trigger DIC","Muscle lysis releases myoglobin, K+, and CK","Myoglobin casts and heme toxicity cause AKI"],"caption":"Direct heat injury and systemic inflammation act together to damage brain, kidney, liver, and the clotting system."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the laboratory picture in exertional heat stroke","text":"A 22-year-old runner collapses late in a summer race, confused, with wet skin and a rectal temperature of 41.2 °C (106.2 °F). CK is very high, K+ is 6.1 mmol/L (reference about 3.5 to 5.0), creatinine is rising, urine is cola colored and heme positive, platelets are low, and PT is prolonged. Confusion above 40 °C (104 °F) defines heat stroke rather than heat exhaustion, and wet skin fits the exertional form. Massive CK with heme-positive urine means rhabdomyolysis, which explains the hyperkalemia and AKI. Low platelets with a prolonged PT point to DIC."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C06","obj":"IN-4.6","title":"Malignant hyperthermia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Malignant hyperthermia is a pharmacogenetic disorder of skeletal muscle. In most susceptible families it is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait caused by mutations in RYR1 on chromosome 19, which encodes the ryanodine receptor type 1, the Ca2+ release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). A minority of cases involve CACNA1S, which encodes the alpha-1 subunit of the dihydropyridine (DHP) receptor. Carriers usually have normal muscle function until they are exposed to a triggering drug. RYR1 mutations also cause central core disease, a congenital myopathy whose patients carry the same susceptibility."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal excitation-contraction coupling explains the defect. An action potential in the T tubule changes the conformation of the voltage-sensing DHP receptor, which is mechanically linked to RYR1 and opens it. Ca2+ leaves the SR, binds troponin C, and allows cross-bridge cycling. Relaxation requires SERCA, the SR Ca2+ ATPase, to pump Ca2+ back into the SR, and both cross-bridge cycling and SERCA use ATP. The mutant RYR1 channel is hypersensitive: volatile halogenated anesthetics (halothane, isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane) and the depolarizing neuromuscular blocker succinylcholine make it open readily and stay open, so cytosolic Ca2+ rises without control."},{"type":"flow","title":"Malignant hyperthermia cascade","steps":["Volatile anesthetic or succinylcholine reaches muscle","Mutant RYR1 opens and stays open","Sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ floods the cytosol","Sustained contraction and continuous SERCA pumping","Huge ATP use raises CO2, O2 use, and heat","Oxygen supply falls behind; lactate accumulates","ATP depletion breaks membranes: K+, myoglobin, CK leak"],"caption":"Every clinical sign traces back to uncontrolled Ca2+ release and the ATP it consumes."},{"type":"p","text":"The signs follow in order. Sustained Ca2+ causes sustained contraction, seen as generalized rigidity or as masseter spasm right after succinylcholine. Cross-bridge cycling and SERCA pumping consume ATP at a huge rate, so aerobic metabolism increases CO2 production and oxygen consumption. End-tidal CO2 therefore rises even though ventilation has not changed (hypercarbia), which is usually one of the earliest signs, together with sympathetically driven tachycardia. As oxygen delivery falls behind demand, glycolysis produces lactate, adding metabolic acidosis to the respiratory acidosis. ATP depletion then disrupts the sarcolemma, releasing K+ (hyperkalemia and arrhythmias), myoglobin, and CK, with the same renal consequences as exertional rhabdomyolysis (C05). Core temperature rise is often a later sign, because heat accumulates only as hypermetabolism continues."},{"type":"p","text":"Malignant hyperthermia is hyperthermia with a normal set point: heat is generated in muscle faster than it can be lost, and the hypothalamus plays no causal role. It must be distinguished from other hypermetabolic states with rigidity and high temperature."},{"type":"table","title":"Malignant hyperthermia compared with related syndromes","cols":["Feature","Malignant hyperthermia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome"],"rows":[["Cause","RYR1 (or CACNA1S) mutation plus volatile anesthetic or succinylcholine","Dopamine D2 receptor blockade (or withdrawal of dopaminergic drugs)","Excess serotonergic activity from one or more serotonergic drugs"],["Site of the defect","Skeletal muscle SR Ca2+ release","Central dopamine pathways","Central and peripheral serotonin receptors"],["Onset","Minutes to hours after exposure","Days","Hours"],["Neuromuscular findings","Generalized rigidity, masseter spasm","Lead pipe rigidity, slowed reflexes","Clonus and hyperreflexia, worse in the legs"],["Typical early clue","Rising end-tidal CO2 under anesthesia","Altered mental status and rigidity","Agitation, tremor, diarrhea, mydriasis"]],"caption":"All three can cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis; the trigger, tempo, and reflex pattern separate them."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During anesthesia, end-tidal CO2 rises steadily although minute ventilation is unchanged and temperature is still normal. What is the mechanism?","explain":"Uncontrolled Ca2+ release drives cross-bridge cycling and SERCA pumping, so muscle ATP use and aerobic CO2 production rise at once. Heat accumulates more slowly, so temperature rises later.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the order of signs and the cascade figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C07","obj":"IN-4.7","title":"Hypothermia and cold injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypothermia is a core temperature below 35 °C (95 °F). It results from heat loss that exceeds production, especially with immersion or wind; from reduced heat production (hypothyroidism, hypoglycemia, adrenal insufficiency, malnutrition); from impaired regulation (hypothalamic lesions, sedatives, and ethanol, which dilates skin vessels and blunts shivering and judgment); and from the limited reserve of infants and older adults. Cold slows every temperature-dependent process, so enzyme reactions, ion pumps, channel gating, and nerve conduction all slow as core temperature falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of hypothermia","cols":["Stage","Core temperature","Main findings and mechanism"],"rows":[["Mild","32 to 35 °C (89.6 to 95 °F)","Maximal shivering, tachycardia, tachypnea, cold diuresis, confusion, ataxia; defenses still active"],["Moderate","28 to 32 °C (82.4 to 89.6 °F)","Shivering stops, bradycardia, atrial fibrillation, Osborn waves, hypoventilation, falling consciousness"],["Severe","Below 28 °C (82.4 °F)","Coma, hypotension, apnea, high risk of ventricular fibrillation and asystole"]],"caption":"Loss of shivering marks the change from a defended state to passive cooling."},{"type":"p","text":"Below about 32 °C (89.6 °F), shivering fails and the hypothalamic controller itself is depressed, so heat production drops when it is most needed and cooling speeds up. Bradycardia comes from slowed phase 4 depolarization in the SA node and is not vagally mediated. PR, QRS, and QT intervals lengthen as conduction and repolarization slow. The Osborn (J) wave is a positive deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment whose amplitude grows as temperature falls; it is attributed to a cold-enhanced transient outward K+ current (Ito) in epicardium more than endocardium, which creates a transmural voltage gradient in early repolarization. The cold myocardium becomes irritable: atrial fibrillation is common in moderate hypothermia, and ventricular fibrillation becomes likely below about 28 °C (82.4 °F), sometimes triggered by handling the patient."},{"type":"p","text":"Other findings follow the same logic. Cold diuresis occurs because peripheral vasoconstriction shifts blood volume centrally, which suppresses antidiuretic hormone (ADH) release, and cold impairs tubular concentrating ability. The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve shifts left, so hemoglobin releases oxygen less readily. Clotting enzymes slow, causing a coagulopathy that routine PT and aPTT may miss because the tests are run at 37 °C (98.6 °F)."},{"type":"p","text":"Frostbite is freezing of tissue. Ice forms first in extracellular fluid, which becomes hyperosmolar and draws water out of cells, so they shrink and suffer electrolyte injury; rapid freezing can also form intracellular ice that ruptures membranes. Much of the damage follows rewarming: injured endothelium, release of thromboxane A2 and prostaglandin F2-alpha, platelet aggregation, and microvascular thrombosis cause progressive dermal ischemia, much like ischemia-reperfusion injury. Fingers, toes, nose, and ears are most exposed because cold-induced vasoconstriction (C03) sacrifices them to protect the core. Superficial frostbite gives erythema and clear blisters; deep frostbite gives hemorrhagic blisters and then dry gangrene."},{"type":"p","text":"Perniosis (chilblains) is a nonfreezing injury. Damp cold above freezing causes prolonged vasospasm of small cutaneous vessels, local hypoxia, and a perivascular lymphocytic inflammatory reaction, producing itchy or burning, tender red to violet papules on the fingers and toes hours after exposure that resolve over one to a few weeks."},{"type":"table","title":"Frostbite compared with perniosis","cols":["Feature","Frostbite","Perniosis (chilblains)"],"rows":[["Exposure","Tissue temperature below freezing","Damp cold above freezing"],["Primary mechanism","Ice crystals and cell dehydration, then endothelial injury and thrombosis on rewarming","Vasospasm of small vessels with perivascular lymphocytic inflammation"],["Lesions","Numb, pale or waxy tissue, blisters, then necrosis and gangrene when deep","Itchy or burning red to violet papules on fingers and toes"],["Course","Tissue loss possible; demarcation over weeks","Usually resolves without tissue loss"]],"caption":"Ice and thrombosis define frostbite; inflammation without freezing defines perniosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Thermoregulation ties several disciplines into one control system. Anatomy places the controller in the hypothalamus and the effectors in the skin: eccrine glands with cholinergic sympathetic innervation, arteriovenous anastomoses, arrector pili muscles, and subcutaneous fat. Biochemistry explains heat production, because shivering, malignant hyperthermia, and brown fat all generate heat by spending the energy of ATP or of the proton gradient without doing external work. Immunology explains fever through IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha, and PGE2, linking the innate response to the hypothalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from which part fails. Fever is a normal controller with a raised target; hyperthermia is a normal target with an overwhelmed controller; heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, and hypothermia injure tissue directly through protein denaturation, ATP depletion, or slowed membrane function. The same downstream injuries, rhabdomyolysis, AKI, DIC, and arrhythmias, recur across these states, so reasoning from mechanism transfers from one question to the next. Temperature thresholds are conventions that vary slightly between sources, so findings such as mental status, sweating, and shivering should be weighed together with the number."}],"summary":["The preoptic (anterior) hypothalamus senses core temperature and drives heat loss; the posterior hypothalamus integrates skin input and drives heat conservation and production.","Anterior hypothalamic lesions cause hyperthermia; posterior lesions cause poikilothermia.","Radiation, conduction, and convection need skin warmer than the environment; evaporation (about 0.58 kcal per gram) needs only a water vapor gradient.","When ambient temperature exceeds skin temperature, sweat evaporation is the only route of heat loss, and high humidity defeats it.","Cold defenses are vasoconstriction, piloerection, shivering (up to about 4 to 5 fold heat production), brown fat UCP1 uncoupling via beta-3 receptors, and slower thyroid driven increases in metabolic rate.","Neonates rely on brown fat because they have high surface area to mass, little insulation, and poor shivering.","In fever, IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha induce COX-2 and PGE2, which raises the set point; in hyperthermia the set point is unchanged and COX inhibition has no effect.","Heat stroke is core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with CNS dysfunction; classic is slow in vulnerable people, exertional is fast in healthy people and causes rhabdomyolysis, AKI, and DIC.","Malignant hyperthermia is uncontrolled RYR1 Ca2+ release after volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine; rising end-tidal CO2 and rigidity precede the temperature rise.","Hypothermia is core temperature below 35 °C (95 °F); shivering stops below about 32 °C (89.6 °F), bradycardia and Osborn waves appear, and VF risk rises below about 28 °C (82.4 °F); frostbite freezes tissue, perniosis does not."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body Temperature Regulation and Fever"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Regulation of body temperature"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Bioenergetics and oxidative phosphorylation, uncoupling proteins"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity, cytokines of inflammation and their systemic effects"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and nutritional diseases, injury by physical agents (thermal injury, hyperthermia, hypothermia)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Fever and disorders of temperature regulation"}]},"IN-5":{"unit":"IN-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01","title":"Skin Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Skin is built from proteins, lipids, and carbohydrates that depend on vitamins and minerals, so it often shows the first visible sign of a nutritional or metabolic defect. This chapter follows collagen synthesis step by step, links vitamin C, copper, niacin, vitamin A, zinc, biotin, riboflavin, and essential fatty acids to specific skin findings, traces vitamin D from the epidermis to the kidney, and explains barrier lipids, sebum, and dermal glycosaminoglycans. It ends with skin signs of systemic metabolism: xanthomas that point to a lipoprotein disorder and the photosensitive blisters of porphyria cutanea tarda. Board questions usually describe a lesion and ask for the enzyme, cofactor, or pathway step behind it.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis and dermis and the dermal-epidermal junction (IN-1)","Protein synthesis on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi, and secretion","Vitamin cofactor roles (NAD+, FAD, pyridoxal phosphate, biotin)","Lipoprotein classes, apolipoproteins, and lipoprotein lipase","Steps of heme synthesis and the enzymes that catalyze them"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal system","how":"The same collagen synthesis steps build bone, tendon, and ligament, so osteogenesis imperfecta and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome affect both skin and skeleton."},{"to":"Endocrine and renal physiology","how":"Vitamin D made in skin becomes a hormone only after hepatic and renal hydroxylation, and the renal step is controlled by PTH, phosphate, and FGF23."},{"to":"Cardiovascular system and lipid metabolism","how":"Xanthomas are visible deposits of the same lipoproteins that drive atherosclerosis and, with severe hypertriglyceridemia, pancreatitis."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Porphyria cutanea tarda is a defect of heme synthesis, and iron overload is one of its strongest triggers."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal system","how":"Malabsorption, carcinoid tumors, and intestinal transport defects such as Hartnup disease and acrodermatitis enteropathica present with skin signs."}],"terms":[{"term":"Procollagen","def":"The triple-helical collagen precursor secreted by fibroblasts that still carries its N- and C-terminal propeptides."},{"term":"Tropocollagen","def":"The collagen molecule left after the propeptides are cleaved, which self-assembles into fibrils."},{"term":"Lysyl oxidase","def":"A copper-dependent extracellular enzyme that converts lysine and hydroxylysine side chains to aldehydes that form covalent cross-links between collagen molecules."},{"term":"Pellagra","def":"The disease of niacin deficiency, classically photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia."},{"term":"Acrodermatitis enteropathica","def":"Zinc deficiency, inherited from a defect in the intestinal zinc transporter or acquired, with dermatitis around body openings and on the hands and feet."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active hormonal form of vitamin D, produced mainly by renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase."},{"term":"Lamellar bodies","def":"Secretory organelles of the stratum granulosum that release the lipids and enzymes that form the extracellular barrier of the stratum corneum."},{"term":"Glycosaminoglycan (GAG)","def":"A long unbranched polysaccharide of repeating disaccharides that binds water and, except hyaluronic acid, is sulfated and attached to a core protein."},{"term":"Xanthoma","def":"A yellow skin or tendon deposit of lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells) that reflects excess plasma lipoproteins."},{"term":"Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD)","def":"The cytosolic heme synthesis enzyme that converts uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III; its deficiency causes porphyria cutanea tarda."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C01","obj":"IN-5.1","title":"Collagen synthesis and the defect at each step","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Collagen is the main structural protein of the dermis, and type I collagen supplies most of its tensile strength. Each molecule is three left-handed alpha chains wound together into a right-handed triple helix, and each chain is a repeat of Gly-X-Y in which X is often proline and Y is often hydroxyproline. Glycine must occupy every third position because it is the only amino acid small enough to fit in the crowded center of the helix."},{"type":"flow","title":"Collagen synthesis from gene to cross-linked fibril","steps":["Pre-pro-alpha chains made on rough ER","Proline and lysine hydroxylated (vitamin C, Fe2+, O2)","Selected hydroxylysines glycosylated (galactose, glucose)","C-propeptides align three chains; triple helix zips","Procollagen packaged in Golgi and secreted","Procollagen peptidases cleave N- and C-propeptides","Tropocollagen self-assembles into fibrils","Lysyl oxidase (copper) cross-links fibrils"],"caption":"The first five steps occur inside the fibroblast and the last three outside it. A failure at any step yields weak or deficient collagen."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the cell, prolyl 4-hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase act on the chains before the helix forms. Both are dioxygenases that use O2 and alpha-ketoglutarate and hold Fe2+ at the active site; ascorbate (vitamin C) reduces the iron back to Fe2+ when it becomes oxidized. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the helix through hydrogen bonding, so underhydroxylated chains fold poorly at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F), and are largely degraded instead of secreted. Hydroxylysine is needed for glycosylation and later for stable cross-links. Disulfide bonds between the C-propeptides bring three chains into register, and the helix then forms from the C-terminus toward the N-terminus."},{"type":"p","text":"Outside the cell, removal of the propeptides converts soluble procollagen to tropocollagen, which packs into staggered fibrils. Lysyl oxidase then oxidizes lysine and hydroxylysine side chains to aldehydes (allysine) that condense into covalent cross-links between molecules. Fibrils without these cross-links assemble but tear easily."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the defect from the failed step","cols":["Failed step","Example","Result in connective tissue"],"rows":[["Prolyl hydroxylation","Scurvy (vitamin C deficiency)","Unstable helix, less collagen secreted; fragile vessels and poor healing"],["Lysyl hydroxylation","Kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (lysyl hydroxylase 1)","Few stable cross-links; hyperextensible skin, scoliosis"],["Triple helix formation","Osteogenesis imperfecta (glycine substitution in type I collagen)","Abnormal or reduced type I collagen; brittle bones, blue sclerae"],["Propeptide cleavage","Dermatosparaxis Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (procollagen N-peptidase)","Disorganized fibrils; very fragile, sagging skin"],["Cross-linking","Menkes disease (copper); lathyrism (lysyl oxidase inhibitor)","Weak fibrils and elastin; vascular and skin fragility"]],"caption":"No common human disease maps to the hydroxylysine glycosylation step. Classic and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome are instead caused by mutations in type V and type III collagen genes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A glycine-to-cysteine substitution in one type I collagen chain still lets the chain be made. Why can a single substituted allele disrupt most of the collagen?","explain":"Each molecule contains two alpha1(I) chains, so with one mutant COL1A1 allele about three of four molecules contain at least one abnormal chain, and a bulky residue in the helix center blocks normal folding of the whole molecule (a dominant negative effect).","check":"Compare with the glycine requirement and the triple helix formation row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C02","obj":"IN-5.2","title":"Scurvy and Menkes disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Scurvy is the clinical result of failed collagen hydroxylation. The body has no way to make ascorbate, and stores are depleted after about one to three months of an intake near zero, so scurvy appears where fruit and vegetable intake is near zero, as in some older adults living alone, people with alcohol use disorder, and those on highly selective diets."},{"type":"p","text":"Every classic finding follows from weak collagen in a particular place. Capillaries around hair follicles have little supporting tissue, so they rupture into perifollicular hemorrhages, often on the legs where hydrostatic pressure is highest. Follicles also become plugged with keratin, and the hair shafts inside them coil into corkscrew hairs. Gum tissue turns over rapidly and is under mechanical stress, so it becomes swollen, spongy, and bleeds easily, especially where teeth are present. New wounds heal poorly because fibroblasts cannot lay down stable collagen, and old scars can break down because collagen is normally being remodeled. Bleeding into joints and under the periosteum causes pain. Platelet counts and clotting tests are normal; the bleeding comes from weak vessel support, not from a coagulation defect. Anemia is common, both from blood loss and because ascorbate normally keeps dietary iron in the absorbable ferrous form."},{"type":"p","text":"Menkes disease reaches the same final step through copper. It is an X-linked recessive disorder of the ATP7A gene, which encodes a copper-transporting ATPase that moves copper out of intestinal cells into the blood and into the secretory pathway of other cells. Copper is absorbed into enterocytes but trapped there, so serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low and copper-dependent enzymes throughout the body run short. Wilson disease involves the related ATP7B transporter in the liver and causes copper overload instead."},{"type":"table","title":"Copper enzymes and the Menkes findings they explain","cols":["Copper enzyme","Normal role","Finding when deficient"],"rows":[["Lysyl oxidase","Cross-links collagen and elastin","Tortuous, fragile arteries; loose skin; bladder diverticula"],["Tyrosinase","Melanin synthesis","Hypopigmented, light-colored hair and skin"],["Thiol oxidation in hair keratin (enzyme not characterized)","Disulfide cross-links in hair keratin","Brittle, twisted (kinky) hair, pili torti"],["Cytochrome c oxidase","Final step of the electron transport chain","Hypotonia, seizures, neurodegeneration"],["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Dopamine to norepinephrine","Autonomic dysfunction"]],"caption":"Affected boys usually present in the first months of life, and the classic severe form is often fatal in early childhood."},{"type":"p","text":"Scurvy blocks an intracellular step, so less collagen is secreted, while Menkes blocks an extracellular step, so collagen is secreted but poorly cross-linked. Both cause vascular fragility, but only Menkes adds kinky hair, hypopigmentation, and neurodegeneration from loss of other copper enzymes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C03","obj":"IN-5.3","title":"Pellagra and the tryptophan to niacin pathway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Niacin (nicotinic acid and nicotinamide, vitamin B3) is the precursor of NAD+ and NADP+, the carriers of hydride ions in hundreds of oxidation-reduction reactions. Deficiency therefore harms tissues with high turnover and high energy demand: the skin, the lining of the gut, and the brain. Pellagra is classically remembered as dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia, and untreated it ends in death."},{"type":"p","text":"The dermatitis is photosensitive. It begins like a sunburn on exposed skin, then becomes dark, thick, scaly, and sharply bordered, as on the backs of the hands and in a band around the neck (Casal necklace). The exact cause of photosensitivity is not fully settled. A leading explanation is that UV-damaged keratinocytes depend on NAD+, the substrate of poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase, to repair DNA, so a low NAD+ supply impairs repair and survival after sun exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"Niacin comes from the diet or is made from tryptophan, and every cause of pellagra either removes tryptophan or blocks this pathway. About 60 mg of tryptophan yields 1 mg of niacin, and the pathway needs vitamin B6 (pyridoxal phosphate for kynureninase) and riboflavin (FAD for kynurenine monooxygenase)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Tryptophan to NAD+ and where each cause acts","steps":["Dietary tryptophan absorbed (lost in Hartnup disease)","Tryptophan diverted to serotonin (carcinoid syndrome)","Kynurenine formed in the liver","FAD-dependent and B6-dependent steps (isoniazid depletes B6)","Quinolinic acid to nicotinic acid mononucleotide","NAD+ and NADP+"],"caption":"Any step that removes tryptophan or its cofactors lowers NAD+ supply."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of pellagra by mechanism","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Corn (maize) based diet","Corn is low in tryptophan and its niacin is bound in a form poorly released by digestion; alkali processing (nixtamalization) frees it"],["Hartnup disease","Autosomal recessive defect of the neutral amino acid transporter (B0AT1, SLC6A19) in the small intestine and proximal tubule; tryptophan is poorly absorbed and lost in urine (neutral aminoaciduria)"],["Carcinoid syndrome","Tumor cells convert a large share of tryptophan, normally about 1%, to serotonin, leaving little for niacin"],["Isoniazid","Forms an inactive compound with pyridoxal and depletes vitamin B6, blocking the B6-dependent step"],["Alcohol use disorder, malabsorption","Low intake and absorption of niacin, tryptophan, B6, and B2"]],"caption":"Dietary niacin bypasses the pathway, so pellagra needs both a low niacin and a low tryptophan supply."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: carcinoid tumor with a rash","text":"A patient with liver metastases from a small intestinal neuroendocrine tumor has flushing, diarrhea, and wheezing, then develops a scaly, dark rash on the backs of the hands and the neck. Step 1: hepatic metastases release serotonin directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing liver inactivation, which explains flushing, diarrhea, and bronchospasm. Step 2: the tumor consumes a large fraction of dietary tryptophan to make serotonin. Step 3: less tryptophan enters the kynurenine pathway, so endogenous niacin and NAD+ synthesis falls. Step 4: NAD+-dependent repair in sun-damaged keratinocytes fails, producing photosensitive pellagra. Urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), the serotonin breakdown product, is elevated, confirming the diversion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with Hartnup disease develop pellagra-like dermatitis even though serum levels of most other amino acids stay near normal?","explain":"Other amino acids are absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides through a separate peptide transporter, which keeps protein nutrition adequate, but free tryptophan uptake through the neutral transporter is reduced and renal loss continues, so niacin synthesis falls when dietary niacin is marginal.","check":"Compare with the Hartnup row of the table and the tryptophan pathway figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C04","obj":"IN-5.4","title":"Skin signs of vitamin A, zinc, biotin, riboflavin, and essential fatty acid deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each of these nutrients has a specific job in renewing epithelium, so the pattern and site of the skin change point to the missing one. Retinoic acid, the active form of vitamin A, binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors that guide keratinocyte differentiation and mucus cell development. Without it, epithelia undergo squamous metaplasia and overkeratinize, so follicles fill with keratin plugs (follicular hyperkeratosis, called phrynoderma) and the skin becomes dry and rough (xerosis). The same defect in the eye causes night blindness, conjunctival xerosis with Bitot spots, and corneal damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Zinc is a cofactor for more than 300 enzymes and a structural part of zinc finger transcription factors, so it is critical where cells divide quickly. Acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form, an autosomal recessive defect in the intestinal zinc transporter ZIP4 (SLC39A4), and it typically appears when an infant is weaned from breast milk, which carries zinc in a more absorbable form. Acquired deficiency follows malabsorption, alcohol use disorder, or zinc-free parenteral nutrition. The dermatitis is red, scaly, and sometimes blistered around the mouth, eyes, and anus and on the hands and feet, with alopecia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste."},{"type":"p","text":"Biotin is the carboxyl carrier for the four carboxylases (acetyl-CoA, pyruvate, propionyl-CoA, and 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA). Deficiency comes from large intakes of raw egg white, whose avidin binds biotin, from inherited biotinidase deficiency, or rarely from long parenteral nutrition without biotin. Loss of acetyl-CoA carboxylase activity limits fatty acid synthesis for barrier lipids, giving a scaly periorificial dermatitis, alopecia, and conjunctivitis that closely resemble zinc deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Riboflavin forms FAD and FMN. Deficiency shows first where epithelium is thin and constantly moving, at the corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis), the lips (cheilosis), and the tongue (glossitis), with seborrheic-like dermatitis. Essential fatty acid deficiency removes linoleic acid, which is built into the special ceramides of the stratum corneum (C06), so the barrier leaks water and the skin becomes dry, scaly, and slow to heal."},{"type":"table","title":"Matching the skin finding to the nutrient","cols":["Nutrient","Key biochemical role","Skin finding","Clues outside the skin"],"rows":[["Vitamin A","Retinoic acid controls epithelial differentiation","Follicular hyperkeratosis, xerosis","Night blindness, Bitot spots"],["Zinc","Enzyme and zinc finger cofactor","Periorificial and acral dermatitis, alopecia, poor healing","Diarrhea, loss of taste, onset at weaning"],["Biotin","Carboxylase cofactor","Periorificial scaly dermatitis, alopecia","Raw egg white intake, conjunctivitis"],["Riboflavin (B2)","FAD and FMN","Angular cheilitis, cheilosis","Glossitis, corneal vascularization"],["Essential fatty acids","Linoleic acid in barrier ceramides and eicosanoids","Dry, scaly dermatitis, poor healing","Fat-free parenteral nutrition, fat malabsorption"]],"caption":"Zinc and biotin deficiency look alike in the skin; history (weaning, egg white, parenteral nutrition) and measured levels separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C05","obj":"IN-5.5","title":"Vitamin D synthesis in skin and what limits it","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) can be made entirely in the body, which is why calcitriol is classed as a hormone. The starting material is 7-dehydrocholesterol, the immediate precursor of cholesterol, held in keratinocyte membranes with the highest amounts in the stratum basale and stratum spinosum. Ultraviolet B light (about 290 to 315 nm) opens its B ring to form previtamin D3, which then rearranges by a heat-dependent isomerization at skin temperature over hours to days into cholecalciferol. Vitamin D binding protein carries it from the skin into the circulation."},{"type":"flow","title":"From 7-dehydrocholesterol to calcitriol","steps":["7-dehydrocholesterol in basal and spinous keratinocytes","UVB opens B ring: previtamin D3","Thermal isomerization: cholecalciferol (D3)","Carried on vitamin D binding protein to liver","Liver 25-hydroxylase: 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol)","Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase (proximal tubule): calcitriol","Calcitriol raises gut Ca2+ and phosphate absorption"],"caption":"Dietary D2 and D3 join the pathway at the liver step. Only the renal step is tightly regulated."},{"type":"p","text":"The two hydroxylations differ in control. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is loosely regulated and depends mostly on substrate supply, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D, with a half-life of about two to three weeks, is the best measure of body vitamin D status. Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is the control point: PTH and low phosphate stimulate it, while FGF23, high phosphate, and calcitriol itself suppress it, and 24-hydroxylase diverts both forms toward inactive products. Because of this control, calcitriol is often normal or even high in early vitamin D deficiency, as rising PTH drives the remaining substrate through the kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that keeps UVB photons from reaching 7-dehydrocholesterol lowers production. Melanin absorbs UVB, so deeply pigmented skin needs several times longer exposure to make the same amount. At high latitude, in winter, and early or late in the day, sunlight passes obliquely through more atmosphere and ozone absorbs most UVB; above about 35 to 40 degrees of latitude little vitamin D is made in winter months. Sunscreen absorbs UVB by design and, applied properly, greatly reduces synthesis, as do clothing, window glass, and staying indoors. Aging reduces epidermal 7-dehydrocholesterol."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged sun exposure does not cause vitamin D toxicity. Once previtamin D3 accumulates, further UV converts it to inactive photoproducts (lumisterol and tachysterol), and excess cholecalciferol in the skin is also photodegraded. Toxicity therefore comes from excess supplementation or from unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylation, as in granulomatous disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two people have the same daily sun exposure: one has deeply pigmented skin in Seattle in winter, the other light skin in the same city in summer. Whose serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D falls, and why does serum calcium change little at first?","explain":"Melanin and low winter UVB both reduce previtamin D3 formation in the first person, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D falls; calcium is defended because lower calcium absorption raises PTH, which increases renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation, bone resorption, and renal calcium reabsorption.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on renal regulation and on factors that block UVB."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C06","obj":"IN-5.6","title":"Barrier lipids, sebum, and dermal glycosaminoglycans","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The permeability barrier of the skin lies in the stratum corneum and is often described as bricks and mortar. The bricks are flattened, dead corneocytes filled with keratin and wrapped in a cross-linked protein envelope; the mortar is stacked lipid sheets (lamellae) between them. These lipids come from lamellar bodies, small secretory organelles that form in the stratum spinosum and empty their contents by exocytosis at the junction of the stratum granulosum and stratum corneum."},{"type":"p","text":"Lamellar bodies secrete precursors together with the enzymes that process them. Beta-glucocerebrosidase converts glucosylceramides to ceramides, acid sphingomyelinase yields further ceramide from sphingomyelin, and phospholipases release free fatty acids from phospholipids. The finished mixture is roughly equal molar amounts of ceramides, cholesterol, and long-chain free fatty acids. Some ceramides carry an ester-linked linoleic acid that helps hold the lamellae together, which is why essential fatty acid deficiency weakens the barrier (C04). The barrier fails in severe Gaucher disease, in which loss of glucocerebrosidase leaves glucosylceramides unconverted."},{"type":"p","text":"Sebum is a different lipid with a different job. Sebaceous glands secrete by the holocrine method, in which whole cells fill with lipid and disintegrate. Androgens drive gland size and output, which explains the rise at puberty. Sebum lubricates hair and skin and adds to surface water resistance. Squalene, a cholesterol precursor, accumulates in sebocytes instead of being converted to cholesterol, and wax esters are also unique to sebum, so both serve as markers of sebaceous lipid. Bacterial lipases from Cutibacterium acnes split triglycerides into free fatty acids on the skin surface."},{"type":"table","title":"Barrier lipid versus sebum","cols":["Feature","Stratum corneum barrier lipid","Sebum"],"rows":[["Source","Lamellar bodies of keratinocytes","Sebaceous glands (holocrine)"],["Main components","Ceramides, cholesterol, free fatty acids","Triglycerides (largest share), wax esters, squalene, small amounts of cholesterol and its esters"],["Location","Between corneocytes","Skin surface and hair follicle"],["Main function","Prevents water loss and entry of substances","Lubrication and surface protection"],["Key regulator","Keratinocyte differentiation","Androgens"]],"caption":"The barrier holds ceramide, cholesterol, and free fatty acid in near equal molar amounts, with ceramides the largest share by mass; triglycerides dominate sebum."},{"type":"p","text":"In the dermis, collagen and elastic fibers sit in a ground substance of glycosaminoglycans that bind large amounts of water and resist compression. Hyaluronic acid is a repeat of glucuronic acid and N-acetylglucosamine; it is the only GAG that is not sulfated and not attached to a core protein, and it is made at the plasma membrane rather than in the Golgi. It is the main water-holding molecule of the dermis and is abundant in loose connective tissue. Dermatan sulfate, a repeat of iduronic acid and N-acetylgalactosamine, is the main sulfated GAG of skin. It is carried on the proteoglycan decorin, which binds collagen fibrils and helps control their diameter and spacing. In Hurler and Hunter syndromes, failure to degrade dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate causes thickened skin, and Hunter syndrome can produce pebbly skin papules over the scapulae."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C07","obj":"IN-5.7","title":"Xanthomas as markers of lipoprotein disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A xanthoma forms when plasma lipoproteins leak through dermal or tendon capillaries and are taken up by macrophages, which fill with lipid droplets to become foam cells. The amount and type of lipoprotein determine where and how fast deposits form, so the morphology of a xanthoma points to a specific disorder."},{"type":"p","text":"Eruptive xanthomas are crops of small yellow papules with a red halo on the buttocks, thighs, and extensor surfaces that appear over days to weeks. They reflect very high triglycerides, typically above about 1,000 mg/dL (about 11 mmol/L), with chylomicrons in the plasma. In familial chylomicronemia (Fredrickson type I), lipoprotein lipase or its activator apoC-II is missing, so chylomicron triglyceride cannot be hydrolyzed in capillary beds. The same chylomicron excess causes milky plasma, lipemia retinalis (creamy white retinal vessels), and acute pancreatitis. Eruptive xanthomas also appear in type V hyperlipidemia and with poorly controlled diabetes, in which insulin deficiency lowers lipoprotein lipase activity. They resolve as triglycerides fall."},{"type":"p","text":"Tendon xanthomas are firm nodules in the Achilles tendons and the extensor tendons of the hands. They are a strong sign of familial hypercholesterolemia (type IIa), most often from mutations in the LDL receptor gene and less often in apoB-100 or PCSK9. Without receptor-mediated uptake, LDL cholesterol stays high from birth, roughly two to three times normal in heterozygotes and higher still in homozygotes, and LDL enters tendons and the arterial wall. Xanthelasma, soft yellow plaques on the eyelids, also occurs with high LDL, but about half of affected people have normal lipid levels, so it is less specific."},{"type":"p","text":"Palmar xanthomas (xanthoma striata palmaris) are yellow deposits in the creases of the palms and fingers and are characteristic of dysbetalipoproteinemia (type III). The apoE2 isoform binds poorly to hepatic remnant receptors, so homozygotes (apoE2/E2) clear chylomicron remnants and IDL slowly. Disease usually appears only with a second factor such as hypothyroidism, obesity, or diabetes, and cholesterol and triglycerides are both raised to similar degrees. Tuberoeruptive xanthomas on the elbows and knees also occur."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading a xanthoma","cols":["Xanthoma","Lipoprotein in excess","Disorder and defect"],"rows":[["Eruptive (buttocks, extensors)","Chylomicrons, VLDL","Type I (lipoprotein lipase or apoC-II deficiency), type V, uncontrolled diabetes"],["Tendon (Achilles, hand extensors)","LDL","Type IIa familial hypercholesterolemia (LDL receptor)"],["Xanthelasma (eyelids)","LDL, or none","Familial hypercholesterolemia; often normal lipids"],["Palmar crease, tuberoeruptive","Chylomicron remnants, IDL","Type III dysbetalipoproteinemia (apoE2/E2)"]],"caption":"Eruptive xanthomas follow triglyceride levels quickly; tendon and palmar xanthomas reflect long-standing cholesterol-rich lipoproteins."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C08","obj":"IN-5.8","title":"Porphyria cutanea tarda","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) is the most common porphyria. It results from reduced activity of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD), the cytosolic enzyme of heme synthesis that removes four carboxyl groups to convert uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III. Most cases (about 75 to 80%) are sporadic, with normal UROD genes; the rest are familial, with one inherited mutant allele that halves enzyme activity in all tissues. In both forms, symptoms appear only when hepatic UROD activity falls below about 20 to 25% of normal, which requires an added trigger."},{"type":"p","text":"The triggers share one mechanism: they increase iron and oxidative stress in hepatocytes. Under those conditions uroporphyrinogen is partly oxidized to uroporphomethene, which inhibits UROD. Alcohol, hepatitis C, HIV, smoking, estrogens (oral contraceptives, hormone therapy), and iron overload, often with hemochromatosis gene (HFE) variants, are the classic factors, and most patients have more than one."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hepatic UROD inhibition to blisters","steps":["Iron and oxidative stress in hepatocytes","Uroporphomethene inhibits hepatic UROD","Uroporphyrinogen accumulates, oxidized to uroporphyrin","Water-soluble porphyrins reach skin through plasma","Porphyrins absorb visible light near 400 nm","Reactive oxygen species damage dermis and basement membrane","Subepidermal blisters, erosions, scarring, milia"],"caption":"Light, not trauma alone, starts the damage, so lesions are confined to sun-exposed skin."},{"type":"p","text":"Uroporphyrinogen and its oxidized form uroporphyrin carry eight carboxyl groups, so they are water soluble and leave in the urine rather than the bile. Absorbing light strongly near 400 nm (the Soret band, visible violet rather than UVB), the excited porphyrin transfers energy to oxygen and generates reactive oxygen species that injure dermal capillaries and the dermal-epidermal junction. Skin on the backs of the hands, forearms, and face becomes fragile, so minor trauma produces erosions and tense subepidermal blisters that heal with scarring and milia (small white keratin cysts). Hypertrichosis over the cheeks and temples, hyperpigmentation, and sclerodermoid plaques are also typical."},{"type":"p","text":"PCT has no acute neurovisceral attacks, because the block is late in the pathway, so the neurotoxic precursors aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen are not raised; acute intermittent porphyria (porphobilinogen deaminase) causes attacks without skin lesions. Urine is reddish brown and shows orange-red fluorescence under a Wood lamp, from raised uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrin. Liver iron is often increased, and PCT carries a higher risk of hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why sunscreen fails in PCT","text":"A man with hepatitis C who drinks heavily has blisters and erosions on the backs of his hands despite daily SPF 50 sunscreen. Step 1: two triggers together raise hepatocyte iron and oxidative stress, so uroporphomethene inhibits hepatic UROD past its threshold. Step 2: uroporphyrin accumulates and deposits in the dermis. Step 3: it absorbs visible light near 400 nm, which sunscreens built to block UVB and UVA pass through, so the reactive oxygen species keep forming and the blisters continue. SPF measures UVB protection only."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The same skin finding can arise at different levels of organization, and the mechanism tells them apart. Collagen fragility can begin with a missing cofactor (vitamin C), a missing metal transporter (ATP7A in Menkes), a structural gene (type I collagen in osteogenesis imperfecta), or a processing enzyme (Ehlers-Danlos subtypes), and the extra findings point to the level: corkscrew hairs and bleeding gums for diet, kinky hair and neurodegeneration for copper, fractures and blue sclerae for type I collagen. Photosensitivity likewise has two unrelated causes in this chapter: impaired NAD+-dependent repair in pellagra, and photochemical generation of reactive oxygen species by porphyrins in PCT, which is why blisters and scarring belong to PCT and not to pellagra."},{"type":"p","text":"The skin also reports on organs far from it. Xanthomas reveal hepatic and capillary lipoprotein handling, PCT reveals hepatic iron and viral infection, vitamin D synthesis links sun exposure to renal hormone production and calcium balance, and periorificial dermatitis can be the first sign of intestinal malabsorption. Skin signs are clues, not proof: zinc and biotin deficiency overlap, xanthelasma often occurs with normal lipids, and laboratory measures such as serum zinc, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, a lipid panel, and urine porphyrins are needed to confirm the mechanism."}],"summary":["Collagen is Gly-X-Y chains hydroxylated in the ER (vitamin C, Fe2+), glycosylated, wound into procollagen, cleaved outside the cell to tropocollagen, and cross-linked by copper-dependent lysyl oxidase.","Scurvy blocks hydroxylation, so collagen is unstable: perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, bleeding gums, poor healing, with normal clotting tests.","Menkes disease (X-linked ATP7A) traps copper in enterocytes, lowering lysyl oxidase and other copper enzymes: kinky hypopigmented hair, fragile vessels, neurodegeneration.","Pellagra is photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia from low NAD+; corn diets, Hartnup disease, carcinoid diversion of tryptophan, and isoniazid-induced B6 loss all reduce niacin.","Vitamin A deficiency causes follicular hyperkeratosis; zinc and biotin cause periorificial dermatitis with alopecia; riboflavin causes angular cheilitis; essential fatty acid loss causes scaly barrier dermatitis.","UVB converts epidermal 7-dehydrocholesterol to previtamin D3; liver 25-hydroxylation makes the storage form and regulated renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation makes calcitriol; melanin, latitude, and sunscreen reduce synthesis.","Lamellar bodies supply ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids for the barrier; sebum is mostly triglycerides, wax esters, and squalene; hyaluronic acid and dermatan sulfate hold dermal water and organize collagen.","Eruptive xanthomas mean very high triglycerides (LPL or apoC-II deficiency), tendon xanthomas mean familial hypercholesterolemia (LDL receptor), and palmar crease xanthomas mean dysbetalipoproteinemia (apoE2).","PCT is reduced hepatic UROD activity, triggered by iron, alcohol, hepatitis C, and estrogen; uroporphyrin absorbs visible light and causes blisters, fragility, and hypertrichosis without neurovisceral attacks."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous proteins (collagen and elastin); Vitamins; Porphyrin metabolism; Lipoprotein metabolism and the hyperlipoproteinemias"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin; Connective tissue ground substance"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation of calcium and phosphate, vitamin D metabolism"}]}});
